514
00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:51.050
Yeah, I mean it was an honor to be there.

515
00:19:51.050 --> 00:19:54.470
And I spent, I spent three plus years there,

516
00:19:54.470 --> 00:19:57.230
being six foot four, 200 plus pounds,

517
00:19:57.230 --> 00:19:59.370
the shortest and lightest guy on the team.

518
00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:01.427
And so I was a little guy for three years.

519
00:20:01.427 --> 00:20:03.580
I had that complex for a while.

520
00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:04.790
When I got home,

521
00:20:04.790 --> 00:20:06.419
guys didn't like that I was jumping on their backs

522
00:20:06.419 --> 00:20:09.090
because I thought my smaller friends could hold me up.

523
00:20:09.090 --> 00:20:12.310
But, so when I was,

524
00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:16.033
when I was recruiting, I wanted to be at once again,

525
00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:18.190
kind of the smaller guy on the team,

526
00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:19.870
I wanted to get some of these big strong guys,

527
00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:21.170
but I really couldn't find.

528
00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:25.100
Those guys just, they graduated and gone on

529
00:20:25.100 --> 00:20:26.315
started careers, started families.

530
00:20:26.315 --> 00:20:28.920
They're all like Goldman Sachs and stuff.

531
00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:30.940
Exactly. Yeah all the Ivy leaguers.

532
00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:33.597
I was like a little state schooler there, but yeah.

533
00:20:33.597 --> 00:20:35.467
They were all, the opportunity costs

534
00:20:35.467 --> 00:20:37.920
had just risen too much since we left Vesper.

535
00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:41.260
So, I was desperate to find those,

536
00:20:41.260 --> 00:20:45.480
those types of individuals and I got, I got one of them,

537
00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:48.140
a guy that rowed at a different club, but a very good club,

538
00:20:48.140 --> 00:20:51.583
Nick. He was a big strong athlete, but the rest,

539
00:20:53.350 --> 00:20:56.230
Tom and Greg, who I recruited as well

540
00:20:56.230 --> 00:20:58.820
on paper, weren't the, weren't those athletes.

541
00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:02.400
But, I think one of the things I learned about what these,

542
00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:03.860
these other rows after that is there,

543
00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:05.560
there's just a difference between,

544
00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:08.550
the best guys and the right guys.

545
00:21:08.550 --> 00:21:09.940
And I was so obsessed with always getting

546
00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:11.470
the best guys, on paper.

547
00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:14.520
It had to be big, tall, strong, good, 2K scores.

548
00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:17.460
How fast you can run with 2000 meters on a rowing machine,

549
00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:19.383
shit doesn't matter out there.

550
00:21:19.383 --> 00:21:21.940
What you need is somebody who's selfless.

551
00:21:21.940 --> 00:21:23.570
who's thinking about everybody else a little bit more than

552
00:21:23.570 --> 00:21:24.680
they're thinking about themselves.

553
00:21:24.680 --> 00:21:27.970
And those types of process-oriented people.

554
00:21:27.970 --> 00:21:29.890
This is stuff I just didn't know in the first race.

555
00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:30.723
Right.

556
00:21:30.723 --> 00:21:32.846
And there wasn't a lot of ocean experience with these guys.

557
00:21:32.846 --> 00:21:35.008
Like on paper, they had amazing CVs

558
00:21:35.008 --> 00:21:38.120
and were super accomplished rowers on the collegiate circuit

559
00:21:38.120 --> 00:21:38.953
or what have you,

560
00:21:38.953 --> 00:21:42.340
but really didn't have any experience at a race like this.

561
00:21:42.340 --> 00:21:44.350
And it's in the documentary it's,

562
00:21:44.350 --> 00:21:45.900
it's Angus who's kind of like,

563
00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:47.190
yeah we'll see how this goes.

564
00:21:47.190 --> 00:21:49.250
Right. He's he's on the British team.

565
00:21:49.250 --> 00:21:53.560
He's kinda casting a glance over, at your team going

566
00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:55.560
yeah they're talking a big game but,

567
00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:57.080
we'll see what happens when we're out there.

568
00:21:57.080 --> 00:22:00.610
Him being this like sort of master rower

569
00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:02.860
who has all of this ocean experience at the time.

570
00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:05.750
Yeah, exactly. We had zero ocean rowing experience.

571
00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:08.700
Now that's not unheard of, for any of these teams.

572
00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:12.150
I mean, most people have never had done an ocean row.

573
00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:14.500
But people like Angus who have grown up with the sea,

574
00:22:14.500 --> 00:22:17.100
he had actually done a row. He rowed the Indian before that.

575
00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:19.590
So we were running against a guy who's put the team together

576
00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:21.840
and has ocean rowing experience.

577
00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:24.550
But yeah, I mean, he was looking at our team and saying,

578
00:22:24.550 --> 00:22:26.150
yep, they can row in flat water

579
00:22:26.150 --> 00:22:28.820
great conditions for short distances really, really well.

580
00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:30.715
But this is the antithesis of that.

581
00:22:30.715 --> 00:22:31.548
Right.

582
00:22:31.548 --> 00:22:34.950
And so, we yeah,

583
00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:37.110
just this kind of naivete that we went in

584
00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:37.943
and just thinking like,

585
00:22:37.943 --> 00:22:39.430
well it's just like a flat water row.

586
00:22:39.430 --> 00:22:42.627
We just have to do it for a longer period of time was,

587
00:22:42.627 --> 00:22:43.470
was detrimental.

588
00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:45.710
You and you soon found out.

589
00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:49.410
I mean pretty, in pretty short shrift things went sideways.

590
00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:52.413
Yeah so, the first thing that happened was,

591
00:22:53.564 --> 00:22:55.970
we're not even a day out from the Canary islands

592
00:22:55.970 --> 00:22:57.290
where the race starts.

593
00:22:57.290 --> 00:22:59.400
We've got 3000 miles of open ocean to go

594
00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:00.960
and we've got a teammate, Nick,

595
00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:03.510
my biggest strongest teammate that's getting sick,

596
00:23:03.510 --> 00:23:04.540
and seasick.

597
00:23:04.540 --> 00:23:06.537
And, we're all, we're all feeling it for sure.

598
00:23:06.537 --> 00:23:08.634
And it just genetically you're gonna

599
00:23:08.634 --> 00:23:10.100
everyone deals with it differently,

600
00:23:10.100 --> 00:23:13.400
but he was just getting absolutely rattled by it.

601
00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:15.330
And he was not just getting ill,

602
00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:17.360
but as he described, it was like,

603
00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:19.420
like someone was squeezing every angle of his head

604
00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:20.420
and then shaking his head.

605
00:23:20.420 --> 00:23:23.020
So he was disoriented and it just got,

606
00:23:23.020 --> 00:23:24.890
it just kept getting worse and worse and worse.

607
00:23:24.890 --> 00:23:29.738
And, we had, we had by day five or so we had,

608
00:23:29.738 --> 00:23:33.261
this teammate that not only wasn't rowing,

609
00:23:33.261 --> 00:23:36.940
but was very, very ill. So we had that issue.

610
00:23:36.940 --> 00:23:39.260
And then we had another issue with Greg

611
00:23:40.240 --> 00:23:42.270
who just didn't wanna be there anymore.

612
00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:44.870
And that was kind of the, kind of the,

613
00:23:44.870 --> 00:23:47.810
the mental prep that I was talking about just, he was,

614
00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:51.700
he was not willing to make the crossing and...

615
00:23:53.460 --> 00:23:56.120
To his credit, he was rowing his shifts all the way through.

616
00:23:56.120 --> 00:23:59.330
So when we had to get an evacuation where

617
00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:02.470
Nick is being evacuated a week into this row,

618
00:24:02.470 --> 00:24:05.840
Greg saw the opportunity to also leave and took it.

619
00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:08.270
So even though he was healthy, he's like, I'm outta here.

620
00:24:08.270 --> 00:24:10.680
Yeah. Yeah, exactly.
This is not going good.

621
00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:12.475
Yep. So he was, he was,

622
00:24:12.475 --> 00:24:14.710
he was scared and it's scary out there.

623
00:24:14.710 --> 00:24:17.900
And, and he was, he,

624
00:24:17.900 --> 00:24:19.950
he saw this opportunity cause we're at this point,

625
00:24:19.950 --> 00:24:21.260
we're anchoring.

626
00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:22.900
So we're not, we're not even moving.

627
00:24:22.900 --> 00:24:25.600
We're anchoring for two days waiting for the sailboat

628
00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:27.890
to basically cover the distance

629
00:24:27.890 --> 00:24:30.386
that we'd covered over the last week to come up to us,

630
00:24:30.386 --> 00:24:32.500
it was gonna take them to two days.

631
00:24:32.500 --> 00:24:36.140
So we're anchoring 600 miles off the coast of Africa

632
00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:37.760
watching the last two days.

633
00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:39.650
We know that boats are just getting further and further

634
00:24:39.650 --> 00:24:43.800
away from us knowing that, I know I'm staying so.

635
00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:46.130
Right. But this idea going into it,

636
00:24:46.130 --> 00:24:48.130
that you're gonna, you're aiming to win

637
00:24:48.130 --> 00:24:49.530
you're aiming to break the world record

638
00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:53.100
and suddenly you're in close to last place anchored,

639
00:24:53.100 --> 00:24:56.550
two guys getting carried off your boat

640
00:24:56.550 --> 00:24:58.840
onto a safety sailboat.

641
00:24:58.840 --> 00:25:01.067
I mean the scene in the documentary of getting those guys

642
00:25:01.067 --> 00:25:05.910
off the boat and onto the sailboat was, unbelievable.

643
00:25:05.910 --> 00:25:07.610
It was much more harrowing than I would have thought

644
00:25:07.610 --> 00:25:08.840
like that would be.

645
00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:10.220
Yeah, it was dramatic.

646
00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:12.870
I mean, we've got two guys that are leaving,

647
00:25:12.870 --> 00:25:15.650
but you've got a sailboat that's,

648
00:25:15.650 --> 00:25:17.500
five times the size of our little rowboat

649
00:25:17.500 --> 00:25:21.570
trying to, collect two people,

650
00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:23.240
one of which can barely even move his arms.

651
00:25:23.240 --> 00:25:26.090
He's so sick from the sea, the open sea.

652
00:25:26.090 --> 00:25:30.290
So, we're having to drop them in with their life jackets on,

653
00:25:30.290 --> 00:25:32.480
but like, and they have to pull them in with a rope.

654
00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:34.830
And at one point we lost the guy who was sick.

655
00:25:34.830 --> 00:25:36.227
He couldn't hold on, Nick.

656
00:25:36.227 --> 00:25:37.697
And he was just drifting out there.

657
00:25:37.697 --> 00:25:38.960
And as you probably saw on the dock,

658
00:25:38.960 --> 00:25:40.570
he was interviewed afterwards and said,

659
00:25:40.570 --> 00:25:42.480
that was the only time in his life he's ever been scared.

660
00:25:42.480 --> 00:25:44.250
And you saw him fighting back those tears

661
00:25:44.250 --> 00:25:46.250
as he's watching the film for the first time.

662
00:25:46.250 --> 00:25:48.127
So he's just bringing them right back to where it was.

663
00:25:48.127 --> 00:25:50.300
And this is, as I've said many times before,

664
00:25:50.300 --> 00:25:52.970
he's one of the most tenacious athletes I've ever met,

665
00:25:52.970 --> 00:25:56.230
I mean this guy was nails and--

666
00:25:56.230 --> 00:25:57.930
It just broke him.
Just broke him.

667
00:25:57.930 --> 00:25:59.700
I mean, that's what the ocean does.

668
00:25:59.700 --> 00:26:03.140
So suddenly your four man boat turns into a two man boat

669
00:26:03.140 --> 00:26:06.150
and then you and Tom decide like, all right,

670
00:26:06.150 --> 00:26:08.610
well we're just gonna, we're gonna just keep going.

671
00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:09.580
Just the two of us.

672
00:26:09.580 --> 00:26:12.480
Yeah. It was not as easy as we're just gonna keep going.

673
00:26:13.750 --> 00:26:16.890
Tom took some convincing. I knew I wasn't gonna leave.

674
00:26:16.890 --> 00:26:20.370
And Tom's one of my closest friends. He was in our wedding.

675
00:26:20.370 --> 00:26:22.040
This is a guy I've worked with for years.

676
00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:24.930
And my last pick on the team, by the way, because,

677
00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:25.857
you saw from the doc, he's not a big guy.

678
00:26:25.857 --> 00:26:27.600
I mean he's tall, but he's skinny,

679
00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:29.650
has trouble putting on weight and muscle.

680
00:26:31.060 --> 00:26:32.170
And--
But he looks,

681
00:26:32.170 --> 00:26:34.900
by the end of the thing, he looks like a marathon runner.

682
00:26:34.900 --> 00:26:36.867
Yeah, yeah, yeah he does.

683
00:26:36.867 --> 00:26:38.840
And he's just, I mean, he didn't,

684
00:26:38.840 --> 00:26:40.900
he couldn't lose any more weight than he had already lost.

685
00:26:40.900 --> 00:26:42.610
I mean, it was starting to get really like, kind of,

686
00:26:42.610 --> 00:26:44.340
that's why he's having back problems by the end of it

687
00:26:44.340 --> 00:26:46.020
and stuff I think it was just, I mean,

688
00:26:46.020 --> 00:26:49.140
I think that that row was eating into like his muscle

689
00:26:49.140 --> 00:26:50.490
and tendons and stuff, but.

690
00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:53.460
He didn't, he wasn't sure he wanted to stay right away,

691
00:26:53.460 --> 00:26:56.180
but he finally got convinced.

692
00:26:56.180 --> 00:27:01.180
And so, he stayed on and once the evacuation had worked,

693
00:27:01.310 --> 00:27:05.370
I just have this vivid memory of just seeing that boat

694
00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:07.520
sailing off literally into the horizon,

695
00:27:07.520 --> 00:27:09.860
heading to the Verde islands.

696
00:27:09.860 --> 00:27:12.110
And we're just, and they're getting smaller

697
00:27:12.110 --> 00:27:13.520
and we're just bobbing, we haven't started rowing.

698
00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:15.822
We're just, the boats just bobbing up and down.

699
00:27:15.822 --> 00:27:18.870
And we're seriously trying to figure out, like, what is our,

700
00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:20.311
like what is our strategy here?

701
00:27:20.311 --> 00:27:23.000
We're 2,400 miles away from the finish,

702
00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:26.110
we're in a boat made for four or five people.

703
00:27:26.110 --> 00:27:27.910
Now gonna be rowed by two.

704
00:27:27.910 --> 00:27:29.620
Yeah. And your goals are out the window.

705
00:27:29.620 --> 00:27:33.999
So you need something else to kind of anchor your focus.

706
00:27:33.999 --> 00:27:36.286
Yeah. Yeah, what was that motivator?

707
00:27:36.286 --> 00:27:39.184
And I think we had a conversation about it.

708
00:27:39.184 --> 00:27:44.130
I think what we decided was to prove everyone wrong

709
00:27:44.130 --> 00:27:45.660
because there were people that said like,

710
00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:48.450
we have a responsibility to get off the boat as well,

711
00:27:48.450 --> 00:27:50.480
because this can't be done with just two people.

712
00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:52.210
This, like they said,

713
00:27:52.210 --> 00:27:54.752
this boat's meant for four or five people.

714
00:27:54.752 --> 00:27:58.230
This is just, this is, this is irresponsible.

715
00:27:58.230 --> 00:28:01.570
And you're other competitors at risk.

716
00:28:01.570 --> 00:28:04.200
Because if you do need a second rescue,

717
00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:07.330
that means resources diverted away from

718
00:28:07.330 --> 00:28:08.220
somebody else's boat.

719
00:28:08.220 --> 00:28:09.290
Exactly.
If they needed help.

720
00:28:09.290 --> 00:28:12.500
Yep. If, one of the safety boats is closest,

721
00:28:12.500 --> 00:28:14.380
they're gonna have to divert their route to get to us,

722
00:28:14.380 --> 00:28:16.510
which could strand other boats.

723
00:28:16.510 --> 00:28:19.260
Also maritime law requires that any other vessel out there,

724
00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:22.600
even if it's a big shipping freighter that has the ability

725
00:28:22.600 --> 00:28:26.280
to rescue us and is close must divert their route to come.

726
00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:27.970
And so there were people that were saying

727
00:28:27.970 --> 00:28:30.400
it's inevitable that that's gonna happen.

728
00:28:30.400 --> 00:28:32.850
So you're gonna just,

729
00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:34.440
you're gonna waste time and resources

730
00:28:34.440 --> 00:28:36.010
and money from somebody.

731
00:28:36.010 --> 00:28:38.510
So it was irresponsible for us to stay on.

732
00:28:38.510 --> 00:28:40.550
I never once saw it that way.

733
00:28:40.550 --> 00:28:43.927
And I mean, when you choose a life of adventure

734
00:28:43.927 --> 00:28:46.170
and endurance rowing you're constantly gonna have

735
00:28:46.170 --> 00:28:47.690
to convince people that what you do

736
00:28:47.690 --> 00:28:49.658
is not irresponsible and selfish,

737
00:28:49.658 --> 00:28:51.563
even though to a certain extent it is.

738
00:28:52.909 --> 00:28:56.400
But that's my big, that's my big argument.

739
00:28:56.400 --> 00:28:58.930
So it was what, what is gonna get us through.

740
00:28:58.930 --> 00:29:00.620
Because one of the things that, and this is,

741
00:29:00.620 --> 00:29:02.660
we're talking about lessons learned from this first crossing

742
00:29:02.660 --> 00:29:04.730
is one of the ones I learned is that,

743
00:29:06.160 --> 00:29:08.740
glory kind of takes you to that start line.

744
00:29:08.740 --> 00:29:11.370
The thought of doing something that no one else has done

745
00:29:11.370 --> 00:29:13.130
and you get these pats on the back

746
00:29:13.130 --> 00:29:14.770
when people hear that you're gonna do this,

747
00:29:14.770 --> 00:29:17.070
and you've got sponsorship that's behind you.

748
00:29:17.070 --> 00:29:19.657
And, a charity that's behind you.

749
00:29:19.657 --> 00:29:20.490
And all of a sudden

750
00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:21.910
you're feeling pretty good about yourself.

751
00:29:21.910 --> 00:29:25.340
But that glory will not get you to the finish.

752
00:29:25.340 --> 00:29:27.200
When you were bobbing up and down in a boat

753
00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:28.540
with just you and one other guy,

754
00:29:28.540 --> 00:29:31.870
600 miles off the coast of Africa with 2,400 miles ago,

755
00:29:31.870 --> 00:29:34.050
those pats on the back and all that stuff

756
00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:36.300
that is not what's gonna get you across.

757
00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:40.640
And we spent too much time kind of soaking that in

758
00:29:40.640 --> 00:29:45.640
and not time trying to understand what gets somebody across

759
00:29:46.090 --> 00:29:50.100
some, an extremely difficult distance such as an ocean.

760
00:29:50.100 --> 00:29:53.143
And what I found out is, it's shame.

761
00:29:54.250 --> 00:29:59.250
It is being more afraid of letting down your teammate,

762
00:30:00.070 --> 00:30:02.930
who's in the boat, your family who's rooting for you,

763
00:30:02.930 --> 00:30:07.320
your charity, your sponsors, your friends, your family,

764
00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:09.660
your community, being more afraid of letting them down

765
00:30:09.660 --> 00:30:11.810
than you are of the elements, than you are of dying,

766
00:30:11.810 --> 00:30:12.653
than you are of,

767
00:30:14.924 --> 00:30:17.260
being scared of getting drenched that night.

768
00:30:17.260 --> 00:30:21.512
And that shame is what gets, what got us through.

769
00:30:21.512 --> 00:30:24.454
It was, what I call later, because shame is not a great

770
00:30:24.454 --> 00:30:27.010
word to be using because as it's a

771
00:30:27.010 --> 00:30:28.580
kind of leveraging of human emotions.

772
00:30:28.580 --> 00:30:30.320
And that's what we did, we leverage each other's

773
00:30:30.320 --> 00:30:31.820
human emotional way that we were more afraid

774
00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:34.300
of letting each other down than we were the elements.

775
00:30:34.300 --> 00:30:37.770
Yeah, at the same time you also had to find a way

776
00:30:37.770 --> 00:30:41.160
to be of mutual support to each other, right.

777
00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:45.740
So you have this experience where you're rowing

778
00:30:45.740 --> 00:30:48.740
and Tom's like, what do you want for breakfast, right.

779
00:30:48.740 --> 00:30:50.380
Like that was, that feels like it was sort of

780
00:30:50.380 --> 00:30:54.273
an inflection point in terms of how you guys

781
00:30:54.273 --> 00:30:56.810
were gonna interrelate to each other and support each other,

782
00:30:56.810 --> 00:30:58.450
but even more broadly,

783
00:30:58.450 --> 00:31:02.140
a lesson in regards to kind of teamwork and leadership.

784
00:31:02.140 --> 00:31:04.192
Generally, yeah it was a huge point

785
00:31:04.192 --> 00:31:05.600
that we didn't notice then.

786
00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:09.242
It wasn't until afterwards that we realized

787
00:31:09.242 --> 00:31:12.100
how pivotal that moment was because we're,

788
00:31:12.100 --> 00:31:13.210
we had been rowing with for

789
00:31:13.210 --> 00:31:14.700
about five or six days by ourselves.

790
00:31:14.700 --> 00:31:16.540
And we were still doing two hours on two hours off,

791
00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:18.666
by the way but instead of being with somebody on deck,

792
00:31:18.666 --> 00:31:20.017
you're by yourself.

793
00:31:20.017 --> 00:31:22.260
So you're rowing two hours while the other guy's getting

794
00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:24.890
some sleep and one of the cabins and then you're rotating.

795
00:31:24.890 --> 00:31:26.530
So there's all this kind of solitude.

796
00:31:26.530 --> 00:31:30.160
I mean, just think about five days of,

797
00:31:30.160 --> 00:31:31.010
we're chatting with each other

798
00:31:31.010 --> 00:31:33.037
every two hours just in passing.

799
00:31:33.037 --> 00:31:36.870
And so we get pounded by this storm

800
00:31:36.870 --> 00:31:38.510
and it's just, it's just relentless.

801
00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:39.413
And this is where you start to think that,

802
00:31:39.413 --> 00:31:40.637
that the ocean is out to get you.

803
00:31:40.637 --> 00:31:43.783
And so for three days, which just get pounded by the storm,

804
00:31:43.783 --> 00:31:45.700
and I'm gonna be honest with you

805
00:31:45.700 --> 00:31:47.403
like at the end of the third day,

806
00:31:48.540 --> 00:31:51.120
I just I wanna quit, I do, I wanna quit.

807
00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:56.120
And I'm mad that I allowed us to stay out there,

808
00:31:56.720 --> 00:31:59.734
and I feel guilty for convincing Tom to stay out there

809
00:31:59.734 --> 00:32:02.350
because he's not doing well.

810
00:32:02.350 --> 00:32:06.328
And that's where we have this kind of without hope or agenda

811
00:32:06.328 --> 00:32:09.920
this breakfast moment where he's about to come on his shift.

812
00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:13.260
It's like, I have like the 6:00 AM to 8:00 AM shift.

813
00:32:13.260 --> 00:32:16.070
And he's about to come on that shift and get ready at eight.

814
00:32:16.070 --> 00:32:19.670
And instead of like coming on, he just asked me like,

815
00:32:19.670 --> 00:32:20.800
what I'd want for breakfast.

816
00:32:20.800 --> 00:32:25.330
And I remember like at that moment being upset with him

817
00:32:25.330 --> 00:32:27.160
for asking such an insensitive question.

818
00:32:27.160 --> 00:32:28.622
Because up to this point,

819
00:32:28.622 --> 00:32:31.800
kind of the guy that was coming on shift would kind of offer

820
00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:34.040
the words of encouragement, empathy.

821
00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:37.150
And, that's what I was living for, my lowest moment,

822
00:32:37.150 --> 00:32:39.870
probably my whole life at this moment is my lowest.

823
00:32:39.870 --> 00:32:43.150
I'm thinking, the dark thoughts of wishing I could quit.

824
00:32:43.150 --> 00:32:45.620
And I wonder if there's a boat close enough and I'm gonna

825
00:32:45.620 --> 00:32:47.090
that's what I'm thinking.

826
00:32:47.090 --> 00:32:48.860
I can't get those thoughts out of my head.

827
00:32:48.860 --> 00:32:50.360
And he asked what I want for breakfast.

828
00:32:50.360 --> 00:32:53.290
And I kind of just play along with it a little bit

829
00:32:53.290 --> 00:32:55.380
and just tell them what my favorite freeze dried meal,

830
00:32:55.380 --> 00:32:56.650
which is the chicken risotto.

831
00:32:56.650 --> 00:32:59.319
And he says, well, I like the spaghetti bolognese.

832
00:32:59.319 --> 00:33:00.797
And we're having this conversation he says,

833
00:33:00.797 --> 00:33:02.210
you want me to make you some coffee?

834
00:33:02.210 --> 00:33:04.090
And I'm like, yeah, sure.

835
00:33:04.090 --> 00:33:05.194
He's like, do you want me to put the

836
00:33:05.194 --> 00:33:06.770
powdered hazelnut creamer?

837
00:33:06.770 --> 00:33:09.680
And I love that stuff. So I said, yeah sure.

838
00:33:09.680 --> 00:33:10.820
And he, he makes a deal.

839
00:33:10.820 --> 00:33:12.010
This is the deal he makes me

840
00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:13.640
says if you row an extra 10 minutes,

841
00:33:13.640 --> 00:33:14.950
like I'll make some food for you.

842
00:33:14.950 --> 00:33:16.860
And I'm aware at this point,

843
00:33:16.860 --> 00:33:18.486
this is 10 minutes of his shift.

844
00:33:18.486 --> 00:33:20.066
Yeah.

845
00:33:20.066 --> 00:33:21.303
An your vulnerable.

846
00:33:21.303 --> 00:33:23.010
Yeah, I'm like a little bit like,

847
00:33:23.010 --> 00:33:24.780
where's he going with this?

848
00:33:24.780 --> 00:33:27.220
But I hate making the food and he knows that

849
00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:28.770
I don't like to do the jet boil,

850
00:33:28.770 --> 00:33:30.500
I'd rather put the muscle into the oars

851
00:33:30.500 --> 00:33:33.060
and he'd rather cook than row.

852
00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:37.745
So, we, I make the deal and, and sure enough,

853
00:33:37.745 --> 00:33:39.880
after some time he, he says,

854
00:33:39.880 --> 00:33:41.360
I got some food for you and we turn around

855
00:33:41.360 --> 00:33:42.193
and I stopped rowing.

856
00:33:42.193 --> 00:33:45.310
And again, we don't even, we're not trying to,

857
00:33:45.310 --> 00:33:46.590
this isn't planned.

858
00:33:46.590 --> 00:33:47.423
But I pulled the oars in

859
00:33:47.423 --> 00:33:48.860
and we just have that breakfast together.

860
00:33:48.860 --> 00:33:50.863
And something really dramatic takes place.

861
00:33:50.863 --> 00:33:55.020
That's, kind of like a subtle but traumatic shift in

862
00:33:55.020 --> 00:33:56.170
kind of the mentality of the boat.

863
00:33:56.170 --> 00:33:57.880
We just, we just hang out.

864
00:33:57.880 --> 00:33:59.860
We're just two buddies at that point,

865
00:33:59.860 --> 00:34:01.580
sharing stories of what's happened to us

866
00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:02.413
the last three nights.

867
00:34:02.413 --> 00:34:03.246
Cause we've been by ourselves

868
00:34:03.246 --> 00:34:05.550
and we're kind of desperate to share with somebody.

869
00:34:05.550 --> 00:34:07.540
So we just kind of just talk and all of a sudden

870
00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:11.440
we could be, we could be at home, talking in one of our,

871
00:34:11.440 --> 00:34:12.900
on our living rooms or something like that.

872
00:34:12.900 --> 00:34:16.550
It's just, there was this comfort that washed over us.

873
00:34:16.550 --> 00:34:18.986
And from that, it was 30 minutes or whatever.

874
00:34:18.986 --> 00:34:20.730
And then we went back onto our shifts.

875
00:34:20.730 --> 00:34:23.030
But from that moment on, for the next 41 days,

876
00:34:23.030 --> 00:34:23.913
we always did breakfast.

877
00:34:23.913 --> 00:34:26.960
It was just something that we could look forward to.

878
00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:30.680
And again, didn't know it at the time, but looking back,

879
00:34:30.680 --> 00:34:32.780
it was just a way for us to kind of re-answer the question

880
00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:34.050
why we were doing this.

881
00:34:34.050 --> 00:34:36.590
It's like have that glory idea at the beginning.

882
00:34:36.590 --> 00:34:38.440
that is not gonna get you through no one.

883
00:34:38.440 --> 00:34:39.640
You don't care anymore,

884
00:34:39.640 --> 00:34:41.810
that people are excited for you to finish,

885
00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:43.310
its just not pulling you through.

886
00:34:43.310 --> 00:34:44.810
But I sure as shit care that the guy

887
00:34:44.810 --> 00:34:47.450
that's sitting 20 feet away from me

888
00:34:47.450 --> 00:34:48.650
is going through a tough time.

889
00:34:48.650 --> 00:34:50.300
And if I could just roll a little harder for him

890
00:34:50.300 --> 00:34:52.110
on this shift and he comes out and he sees

891
00:34:52.110 --> 00:34:54.800
that I put four miles in instead of three,

892
00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:56.840
that'll make him happy and he'd wanna do the same

893
00:34:56.840 --> 00:34:58.420
because he won't want to let me down.

894
00:34:58.420 --> 00:35:00.290
So it was just as back and forth for the next 41 days

895
00:35:00.290 --> 00:35:02.620
of just trying to live up to the other person.

896
00:35:02.620 --> 00:35:03.630
And it was just,

897
00:35:03.630 --> 00:35:05.280
the ante just kept getting higher and higher.

898
00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:07.970
And breakfast was a way to kind of

899
00:35:07.970 --> 00:35:09.310
realign ourselves every 24 hours.

900
00:35:09.310 --> 00:35:11.930
I mean if your just ships passing in the night.

901
00:35:11.930 --> 00:35:15.380
And the only exchange that you have is when one's getting,

902
00:35:15.380 --> 00:35:18.300
off the chair and the other one's getting in,

903
00:35:18.300 --> 00:35:19.860
you're not gonna be in sync.

904
00:35:19.860 --> 00:35:24.070
Like you've got to be aligned, emotionally

905
00:35:24.070 --> 00:35:26.320
and check in with each other when you're working

906
00:35:26.320 --> 00:35:28.860
towards this goal otherwise there's no way.

907
00:35:28.860 --> 00:35:31.550
I mean, the ship would quite literally capsize.

908
00:35:31.550 --> 00:35:32.983
Yeah. It's exactly what it was.

909
00:35:32.983 --> 00:35:36.080
And that kind of realignment was,

910
00:35:36.080 --> 00:35:38.550
and that breakfast was our way of just kind of

911
00:35:39.590 --> 00:35:42.316
realigning ourselves and kind of reminding ourselves

912
00:35:42.316 --> 00:35:43.530
of why we're doing this.

913
00:35:43.530 --> 00:35:45.370
By looking someone else in the face that you could see,

914
00:35:45.370 --> 00:35:47.860
man the last 24 hours beat you to shit.

915
00:35:47.860 --> 00:35:50.540
You can see it in his face, and he could see it in mine.

916
00:35:50.540 --> 00:35:52.310
And I was so proud of him,

917
00:35:52.310 --> 00:35:54.763
because Tom is, to this day,

918
00:35:54.763 --> 00:35:56.490
he's my close friend, we work together.

919
00:35:56.490 --> 00:36:00.063
But I just, he had no business being out there.

920
00:36:01.520 --> 00:36:03.600
We had, we worked together in old company.

921
00:36:03.600 --> 00:36:06.580
One of the guys I worked there actually,

922
00:36:06.580 --> 00:36:09.980
our boss said, you should cut Tom from the team.

923
00:36:09.980 --> 00:36:11.873
He's not, he's not built for this.

924
00:36:12.810 --> 00:36:15.740
And I remember considering it because

925
00:36:15.740 --> 00:36:17.940
the guy that was saying this,

926
00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:21.113
our boss was a former Olympian. He knew rowing.

927
00:36:22.121 --> 00:36:23.960
And I'm so glad I didn't,

928
00:36:23.960 --> 00:36:27.890
because he on paper was not the right guy,

929
00:36:27.890 --> 00:36:29.800
but absolutely the right guy for this boat.

930
00:36:29.800 --> 00:36:32.360
And I just was so afraid to let him down.

931
00:36:32.360 --> 00:36:34.540
And I knew that he needed me to put the muscle in and I did.

932
00:36:34.540 --> 00:36:36.480
And he was so afraid to let me down.

933
00:36:36.480 --> 00:36:38.220
He cooked for me and he'd,

934
00:36:38.220 --> 00:36:39.657
he'd get bandages out for me when I,

935
00:36:39.657 --> 00:36:40.760
when I had infection.

936
00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:42.100
And he's like, cause I know you're not gonna take care

937
00:36:42.100 --> 00:36:43.770
of yourself Jay, so let me help you do it.

938
00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:45.850
It's just this, this brotherhood.

939
00:36:45.850 --> 00:36:49.253
And it was,

940
00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:52.071
quite frankly,

941
00:36:52.071 --> 00:36:54.140
kind of one of the most amazing things ever happened to me.

942
00:36:54.140 --> 00:36:57.990
Yeah. You end up passing a bunch of boats,

943
00:36:57.990 --> 00:36:58.920
you ended up finishing,

944
00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:02.660
you going for, you go from almost last to 11th. Right.

945
00:37:02.660 --> 00:37:05.018
You get 11th in that race took you 51 days.

946
00:37:05.018 --> 00:37:05.851
Yeah.

947
00:37:05.851 --> 00:37:09.588 line:15%
But I feel like that was you getting a masters in,

948
00:37:09.588 --> 00:37:12.373 line:15%
what you needed to know to go back and,

949
00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:18.950
have a more, a more perfect attempt.

950
00:37:18.950 --> 00:37:21.770
And it had nothing to do with how you trained for it.

951
00:37:21.770 --> 00:37:23.500
Like all the stuff that you would think like, okay,

952
00:37:23.500 --> 00:37:25.580
back to the drawing board, like we need to change this,

953
00:37:25.580 --> 00:37:26.413
we need to change that.

954
00:37:26.413 --> 00:37:29.650
It was all about the emotional piece, right?

955
00:37:29.650 --> 00:37:33.340
Like extrapolating on that breakfast experience

956
00:37:33.340 --> 00:37:36.199
and the guy who, was healthy, but got, decided to leave.

957
00:37:36.199 --> 00:37:38.890
Like you go back to the drawing board and you're like,

958
00:37:38.890 --> 00:37:42.410
I got to solve this through a different lens.

959
00:37:42.410 --> 00:37:44.980
Yeah. And just even to take it once,

960
00:37:44.980 --> 00:37:47.623
take a step back is that,

961
00:37:48.736 --> 00:37:50.570
I didn't have no plans on doing the second time.

962
00:37:50.570 --> 00:37:53.120
Even if I didn't win, I was,

963
00:37:53.120 --> 00:37:54.313
I thought this is a one and done type of thing.

964
00:37:54.313 --> 00:37:58.470
I mean, so few people have ever even attempted a second one.

965
00:37:58.470 --> 00:37:59.740
Most of those people are because

966
00:37:59.740 --> 00:38:01.390
they didn't finish the first time.

967
00:38:01.390 --> 00:38:03.780
they made it so many days they had to call out.

968
00:38:03.780 --> 00:38:05.360
They don't wanna try to do it again.

969
00:38:05.360 --> 00:38:06.950
Like for us to finish was still a great thing.

970
00:38:06.950 --> 00:38:09.990
But like, I couldn't shake the sense of failure.

971
00:38:09.990 --> 00:38:13.493
And I even, I say this in the doc, but it's so true.

972
00:38:13.493 --> 00:38:17.300
It's like, I remember the juxtaposition between

973
00:38:17.300 --> 00:38:19.190
my emotional state and Tom's emotional state

974
00:38:19.190 --> 00:38:20.650
as we are coming into the finish line,

975
00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:22.970
seeing everybody on the cliffs,

976
00:38:22.970 --> 00:38:24.370
seeing our families that we come in and see was just,

977
00:38:24.370 --> 00:38:28.830
he was giddy. He was beside himself. He was so excited.

978
00:38:28.830 --> 00:38:33.479
And I did, I felt like I was acting to be as giddy as him.

979
00:38:33.479 --> 00:38:35.650
I did feel that way.

980
00:38:35.650 --> 00:38:36.483
Because I think--

981
00:38:36.483 --> 00:38:37.316
Meaning you didn't really feel that way.

982
00:38:37.316 --> 00:38:39.576
You felt like you had failed.

983
00:38:39.576 --> 00:38:41.406
Or what was the, what was your interior?

984
00:38:41.406 --> 00:38:42.517
Yeah, I don't know if I,

985
00:38:42.517 --> 00:38:45.240
I don't know if I would have defined it as failure yet.

986
00:38:45.240 --> 00:38:48.850
Although I did later and I still do today as a,

987
00:38:48.850 --> 00:38:51.530
as a captain, as a leader, I had failed that team.

988
00:38:51.530 --> 00:38:53.890
But at that moment, I just felt this idea.

989
00:38:53.890 --> 00:38:56.270
Like, I felt like maybe I was only halfway done.

990
00:38:56.270 --> 00:38:57.300
And from like this,

991
00:38:57.300 --> 00:38:59.468
that I was gonna have to do another one.

992
00:38:59.468 --> 00:39:01.557
And Tom, I knew it was never gonna do another one.

993
00:39:01.557 --> 00:39:02.837
It wasn't it for him.

994
00:39:02.837 --> 00:39:04.623
He had nothing left to prove.

995
00:39:05.638 --> 00:39:08.238
You see, you see the pictures of him, you know that.

996
00:39:09.350 --> 00:39:13.570
But there we both, we both entered for different reasons,

997
00:39:13.570 --> 00:39:16.050
and he had proven everything he needed to prove to himself

998
00:39:16.050 --> 00:39:17.440
and to anybody else to doubt him.

999
00:39:17.440 --> 00:39:19.550
But I had something left to do.

1000
00:39:19.550 --> 00:39:22.840
So that's, that was what happened

1001
00:39:22.840 --> 00:39:26.580
is stepping off as I didn't feel the closure.

1002
00:39:26.580 --> 00:39:27.938
Well, let's, let's spend a few minutes there

1003
00:39:27.938 --> 00:39:30.080
and maybe this is a good time to like,

1004
00:39:30.080 --> 00:39:32.790
take it back a little bit because I'm interested in,

1005
00:39:32.790 --> 00:39:35.810
in that internal drive that you had.

1006
00:39:35.810 --> 00:39:39.660
As somebody who had been successful as an athlete,

1007
00:39:39.660 --> 00:39:42.980
but had never really had the victories that you sought.

1008
00:39:42.980 --> 00:39:44.200
Right, like it was always like,

1009
00:39:44.200 --> 00:39:45.730
you were the guy who came in second

1010
00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:48.680
or had an opportunity to do something great,

1011
00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:50.560
but something would happen and you wouldn't quite

1012
00:39:50.560 --> 00:39:51.700
connect with it.

1013
00:39:51.700 --> 00:39:55.040
So there's this, kind of cauldron inside of you,

1014
00:39:55.040 --> 00:39:57.462
this sense that you have something to express,

1015
00:39:57.462 --> 00:40:00.620
and yet kind of repeated frustrations with

1016
00:40:00.620 --> 00:40:03.380
your ability to execute on that.

1017
00:40:03.380 --> 00:40:05.840
Yeah. It was wall full of silver medals

1018
00:40:05.840 --> 00:40:06.680
as I like to call it.

1019
