1267
00:49:09.450 --> 00:49:11.690
I've got one year under my belt as a master's in geology.

1268
00:49:11.690 --> 00:49:13.722
You know a little thing about rocks.

1269
00:49:13.722 --> 00:49:15.373
Rocks for jocks, you know?

1270
00:49:16.320 --> 00:49:19.710
And I, so I was able to run another year college there.

1271
00:49:19.710 --> 00:49:22.040
So you did a year there and then you went back.

1272
00:49:22.040 --> 00:49:24.450
Yeah so I got a second year rowing at Sonoma,

1273
00:49:24.450 --> 00:49:25.600
just staying eligible.

1274
00:49:25.600 --> 00:49:26.433
I see.

1275
00:49:26.433 --> 00:49:27.410
There just cause I knew I needed,

1276
00:49:27.410 --> 00:49:31.120
I needed to get more, basically more strokes under my belt.

1277
00:49:31.120 --> 00:49:32.870
So then I went back to Vesper the next year

1278
00:49:32.870 --> 00:49:33.910
and made the year round team.

1279
00:49:33.910 --> 00:49:36.840
Yeah. So what did you do during that year in Sonoma

1280
00:49:36.840 --> 00:49:38.510
that allowed you to make that leap?

1281
00:49:38.510 --> 00:49:40.120
Was it just more time in the boat and experience

1282
00:49:40.120 --> 00:49:42.310
and just the grind?

1283
00:49:42.310 --> 00:49:44.014
It was that, but I was also,

1284
00:49:44.014 --> 00:49:47.890
this is my journey and to becoming a better leader.

1285
00:49:47.890 --> 00:49:49.210
That first year at Sonoma,

1286
00:49:49.210 --> 00:49:51.140
I had a chip on my shoulder,

1287
00:49:51.140 --> 00:49:53.280
I was too good for this sport kind of

1288
00:49:53.280 --> 00:49:54.597
sometimes too good for these guys.

1289
00:49:54.597 --> 00:49:59.290
And I played baseball and I was in an elite athlete

1290
00:49:59.290 --> 00:50:02.250
and I'm getting to this sport and this is a rag tag team

1291
00:50:02.250 --> 00:50:04.358
of hand-me-down equipment and all this kind of stuff.

1292
00:50:04.358 --> 00:50:07.440
And I made good friends with a couple of the guys,

1293
00:50:07.440 --> 00:50:08.310
but for everybody else,

1294
00:50:08.310 --> 00:50:10.660
it was just kinda like the mentality that my dad has

1295
00:50:10.660 --> 00:50:13.550
was like, prove to me that you're worthy

1296
00:50:13.550 --> 00:50:16.780
of being in this boat with me.

1297
00:50:16.780 --> 00:50:18.390
When I get to Vesper for that one summer

1298
00:50:18.390 --> 00:50:20.370
and between those two years at Sonoma,

1299
00:50:20.370 --> 00:50:21.890
I realized that that is not the type,

1300
00:50:21.890 --> 00:50:23.970
the type of leaders on Vesper were different guys.

1301
00:50:23.970 --> 00:50:26.233
They were, they were selfless guys.

1302
00:50:27.170 --> 00:50:29.023
They weren't necessarily even the biggest and fastest guys

1303
00:50:29.023 --> 00:50:31.010
that were well-respected on that team.

1304
00:50:31.010 --> 00:50:32.970
They were just these guys that everyone can count on,

1305
00:50:32.970 --> 00:50:35.530
no matter what happened when they were in the boat,

1306
00:50:35.530 --> 00:50:37.070
the boat went faster.

1307
00:50:37.070 --> 00:50:39.454
It had nothing to do with how fast I could pull 2000 meters

1308
00:50:39.454 --> 00:50:42.320
on the rowing machine or how much they could bench press

1309
00:50:42.320 --> 00:50:44.190
or or leg press.

1310
00:50:44.190 --> 00:50:47.660
It was just when, when, when Jaime went in the boat,

1311
00:50:47.660 --> 00:50:49.380
the boat was faster when you took them out of the boat,

1312
00:50:49.380 --> 00:50:51.520
whoever you put in, it went slower.

1313
00:50:51.520 --> 00:50:54.350
And I started to realize that there was this sense of

1314
00:50:55.730 --> 00:50:58.560
when you were, someone was in the boat that you respected,

1315
00:50:58.560 --> 00:51:00.520
you wanted to pull harder for them.

1316
00:51:00.520 --> 00:51:01.940
They didn't have to pull harder.

1317
00:51:01.940 --> 00:51:04.780
It's just the, it's the seven other guys in the boat

1318
00:51:04.780 --> 00:51:08.350
that said, hey, Jaime is in this boat.

1319
00:51:08.350 --> 00:51:11.600
We love this guy. And let's pull hard for him.

1320
00:51:11.600 --> 00:51:13.860
And everybody, and that's the force multiplier

1321
00:51:13.860 --> 00:51:16.100
of rowing is that you don't need to pull harder

1322
00:51:16.100 --> 00:51:18.640
you just need to leverage the human emotion of everyone else

1323
00:51:18.640 --> 00:51:19.680
so that they pull harder.

1324
00:51:19.680 --> 00:51:22.720
But you also have to work like a single organism, right?

1325
00:51:22.720 --> 00:51:25.900
There's like this heavy symbiosis that has to occur.

1326
00:51:25.900 --> 00:51:28.489
So if somebody is way stronger than everyone else,

1327
00:51:28.489 --> 00:51:29.700
that's great.

1328
00:51:29.700 --> 00:51:33.380
But if the energy's off and you're not kind of congealed

1329
00:51:33.380 --> 00:51:35.970
into this unit, it's not gonna work, right.

1330
00:51:35.970 --> 00:51:38.250
I mean, I say that as somebody who knows very little

1331
00:51:38.250 --> 00:51:40.670
about rowing but it seems like that would be the case.

1332
00:51:40.670 --> 00:51:41.503
Yeah.

1333
00:51:41.503 --> 00:51:44.800
I mean, complete interdependency and swinging together.

1334
00:51:44.800 --> 00:51:47.250
And this idea that when you, the boat

1335
00:51:47.250 --> 00:51:49.820
and that's, what's great about rowing and,

1336
00:51:49.820 --> 00:51:52.970
is when you are rowing well together, the boat gets lighter

1337
00:51:52.970 --> 00:51:53.960
it gets easier.

1338
00:51:53.960 --> 00:51:56.390
You can actually feel like you could row that way forever.

1339
00:51:56.390 --> 00:51:57.700
And so that's what you're aiming for,

1340
00:51:57.700 --> 00:52:01.073
every rower is trying to feel that swing, that flow,

1341
00:52:02.050 --> 00:52:05.570
that means that everybody is rowing together.

1342
00:52:05.570 --> 00:52:09.340
And so that's, that was the second piece of it is that

1343
00:52:10.190 --> 00:52:14.130
great rowers could assimilate to the rest of the team.

1344
00:52:14.130 --> 00:52:16.530
They didn't force people to row like them.

1345
00:52:16.530 --> 00:52:20.367
They gave up that individual ego for the collective ego

1346
00:52:20.367 --> 00:52:22.600
and they rode like everybody else.

1347
00:52:22.600 --> 00:52:23.690
Right.

1348
00:52:23.690 --> 00:52:26.780
So you don't hear of these great individual champions.

1349
00:52:26.780 --> 00:52:29.940
I think it harms the sport in terms of

1350
00:52:29.940 --> 00:52:31.780
like mainstream visibility,

1351
00:52:31.780 --> 00:52:35.630
because we don't have big names that we can rally behind

1352
00:52:35.630 --> 00:52:37.580
because it's such a team thing.

1353
00:52:37.580 --> 00:52:40.760
But I think in the conversation around like

1354
00:52:40.760 --> 00:52:44.100
great endurance athletes, like nobody's harder than rowers.

1355
00:52:44.100 --> 00:52:48.310
Like these guys, basically just destroy themselves.

1356
00:52:48.310 --> 00:52:51.000
Like you hear about cyclists and marathon runners,

1357
00:52:51.000 --> 00:52:52.180
et cetera, and all of that.

1358
00:52:52.180 --> 00:52:55.610
But like rowers are like the anonymous heroes

1359
00:52:55.610 --> 00:52:57.950
in the endurance world who grind like

1360
00:52:57.950 --> 00:53:00.000
no other athletes I've ever met.

1361
00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:00.833
That's well said.

1362
00:53:00.833 --> 00:53:02.310
I've been in running a lot longer than you have

1363
00:53:02.310 --> 00:53:04.016
and I haven't ever said it that well.

1364
00:53:04.016 --> 00:53:06.700
I feel like, I feel like, I feel like they don't get it

1365
00:53:06.700 --> 00:53:09.640
they don't get enough deserved kind of attention

1366
00:53:09.640 --> 00:53:11.860
and accolades for what goes into

1367
00:53:11.860 --> 00:53:13.200
being great at that sport.

1368
00:53:13.200 --> 00:53:14.350
But I also, I agree,

1369
00:53:14.350 --> 00:53:16.810
and I also think that's what makes the sport so great.

1370
00:53:16.810 --> 00:53:20.110
And there's a certain allure to that.

1371
00:53:20.110 --> 00:53:23.790
And I think you, when you hear about great rowing teams,

1372
00:53:23.790 --> 00:53:25.280
that's exactly how they're called.

1373
00:53:25.280 --> 00:53:30.075
They're called the crew of 87 or, Vespers crew of 06

1374
00:53:30.075 --> 00:53:31.230
or whatever it is.

1375
00:53:31.230 --> 00:53:33.230
It's not LeBron and Michael Jordan.

1376
00:53:33.230 --> 00:53:35.360
Exactly. It's this, it's the crew.

1377
00:53:35.360 --> 00:53:38.680
And that's the word is, the crew, that's the noun.

1378
00:53:38.680 --> 00:53:40.530
Rowing's the verb and crews the noun.

1379
00:53:41.668 --> 00:53:44.550
And so, but there is something,

1380
00:53:44.550 --> 00:53:47.850
if you can get over that individual kind of ego of it all,

1381
00:53:47.850 --> 00:53:50.160
which by the way, is very difficult to do.

1382
00:53:50.160 --> 00:53:50.993
If you can't--

1383
00:53:50.993 --> 00:53:54.680
Cause you need ego, but it's the humility piece that,

1384
00:53:54.680 --> 00:53:57.170
that like is the distinguishing factor

1385
00:53:57.170 --> 00:53:59.110
to make a champion crew.

1386
00:53:59.110 --> 00:54:01.470
And by the way, the people that will know who did it

1387
00:54:01.470 --> 00:54:03.440
is the rest of the team.

1388
00:54:03.440 --> 00:54:06.870
Cause you're the, you're spending hours and hours and

1389
00:54:07.830 --> 00:54:11.080
thousands of meters rowing together,

1390
00:54:11.080 --> 00:54:13.020
but you're also spending time on the docks.

1391
00:54:13.020 --> 00:54:15.170
You're spending time together.

1392
00:54:15.170 --> 00:54:18.143
I mean, Vesper was a work hard play hard place.

1393
00:54:19.060 --> 00:54:21.640
We rowed twice a day together with two practices a day,

1394
00:54:21.640 --> 00:54:24.140
usually a weightlifting session in the middle.

1395
00:54:24.140 --> 00:54:26.270
And Saturday night because there was no practice on Sunday

1396
00:54:26.270 --> 00:54:27.250
that's the night you go out.

1397
00:54:27.250 --> 00:54:28.083
Yeah sure.

1398
00:54:28.083 --> 00:54:28.940
So you're going out,

1399
00:54:28.940 --> 00:54:31.590
with 10 other guys and you taking care of each other,

1400
00:54:31.590 --> 00:54:33.113
Philly's not an easy place.

1401
00:54:34.840 --> 00:54:37.060
And when 12 big guys going to a bar

1402
00:54:37.060 --> 00:54:38.680
or something like that trouble ensues.

1403
00:54:38.680 --> 00:54:41.060
And, but it's the guys that take care of each other.

1404
00:54:41.060 --> 00:54:42.990
It's like a seal team six kind of vibe, right?

1405
00:54:42.990 --> 00:54:47.048
Yeah. Except for, we're not as tough as those guys.

1406
00:54:47.048 --> 00:54:49.500
But it is, it is definitely that way.

1407
00:54:49.500 --> 00:54:52.100
It's this, it's this idea.

1408
00:54:52.100 --> 00:54:54.270
And how there's this guy, there was this guy at Vesper,

1409
00:54:54.270 --> 00:54:56.370
I know we're getting a little on a little bit of a tangent,

1410
00:54:56.370 --> 00:54:57.203
but I think it's important.

1411
00:54:57.203 --> 00:54:59.260
There was this guy, the first summer Vesper's really

1412
00:54:59.260 --> 00:55:02.050
what brought me to go to Sonoma the second year.

1413
00:55:02.050 --> 00:55:04.350
Don wiper never forget him, went to Dartmouth.

1414
00:55:06.451 --> 00:55:09.640
I'm there, I'm a, wide-eyed novice rower.

1415
00:55:09.640 --> 00:55:10.880
I'm pretty sure that these guys think,

1416
00:55:10.880 --> 00:55:12.230
why am I even on this team?

1417
00:55:13.152 --> 00:55:15.610
They're being nice to me, but they're not,

1418
00:55:15.610 --> 00:55:17.690
they're not including me in a lot of stuff

1419
00:55:17.690 --> 00:55:19.660
and I probably would have done the same.

1420
00:55:19.660 --> 00:55:22.960
But you weren't getting beat up by some Winkle vie.

1421
00:55:22.960 --> 00:55:26.730
No, no. In fact, I was usually the enforcer down the road.

1422
00:55:26.730 --> 00:55:29.980
I was the, I was the guy doing all the, the rough housing.

1423
00:55:29.980 --> 00:55:32.170
If we got into trouble out there, as I'm sure we did,

1424
00:55:32.170 --> 00:55:34.572
those stories are for another time.

1425
00:55:34.572 --> 00:55:36.060
But this guy, Don,

1426
00:55:36.060 --> 00:55:39.260
he was the stroke seat of the top boat at Vesper.

1427
00:55:39.260 --> 00:55:40.490
And he's not the biggest guy.

1428
00:55:40.490 --> 00:55:45.010
Doesn't have the fastest 2K, and no one can beat him.

1429
00:55:45.010 --> 00:55:46.670
No one can beat him out of that spot.

1430
00:55:46.670 --> 00:55:48.260
And the, the stroke seat, which is eight seat,

1431
00:55:48.260 --> 00:55:49.680
that's like the quarterback of the team.

1432
00:55:49.680 --> 00:55:51.290
That's, the guy.

1433
00:55:51.290 --> 00:55:52.770
You were largely considered to be

1434
00:55:52.770 --> 00:55:55.090
the best rower on the boat.

1435
00:55:55.090 --> 00:55:57.140
He's not the biggest guy, not the strongest guy.

1436
00:55:57.140 --> 00:55:58.140
Doesn't come from the best school.

1437
00:55:58.140 --> 00:56:00.640
Dartmouth's a good school but in the IVs,

1438
00:56:00.640 --> 00:56:02.620
they get up, they get beat up by the Princeton,

1439
00:56:02.620 --> 00:56:05.390
Harvard's and Yales and no one could beat him.

1440
00:56:05.390 --> 00:56:07.960
And one day I just asked him, like, how do you do it?

1441
00:56:07.960 --> 00:56:09.200
I'm, I'm pretty much, I'm pretty certain

1442
00:56:09.200 --> 00:56:11.170
this is my last summer first and last summer at Vesper.

1443
00:56:11.170 --> 00:56:13.880
Might as well get as much as I can out of this.

1444
00:56:13.880 --> 00:56:15.300
I asked him one day in the weight room,

1445
00:56:15.300 --> 00:56:16.980
I was like, how do you do it?

1446
00:56:16.980 --> 00:56:19.650
Like, what are you doing to keep you there?

1447
00:56:19.650 --> 00:56:21.930
Now, one of the things is to find out

1448
00:56:21.930 --> 00:56:24.340
who as an individual is faster in a rowing boat,

1449
00:56:24.340 --> 00:56:26.350
you row two boats for a length of time

1450
00:56:26.350 --> 00:56:28.250
called two-minute side-by-side.

1451
00:56:28.250 --> 00:56:31.630
You measure the distance that they got from each other.

1452
00:56:31.630 --> 00:56:33.320
You record that distance, a coach as well.

1453
00:56:33.320 --> 00:56:35.490
Then you take one person from each boat,

1454
00:56:35.490 --> 00:56:38.570
you swap them and you row them again the same distance.

1455
00:56:38.570 --> 00:56:40.440
And you see what the change was.

1456
00:56:40.440 --> 00:56:43.050
That's how you find out how the individual

1457
00:56:43.050 --> 00:56:45.040
is adding to the speed of the boat and Don.

1458
00:56:45.040 --> 00:56:46.260
And that's called seat racing.

1459
00:56:46.260 --> 00:56:49.210
And Don would never lose a seat race, never.

1460
00:56:49.210 --> 00:56:50.940
This guy, no matter who he was going against,

1461
00:56:50.940 --> 00:56:52.350
he would always beat him.

1462
00:56:52.350 --> 00:56:54.830
And I said like, how you always win the seat races?

1463
00:56:54.830 --> 00:56:56.370
He says, oh, I just set the boat up.

1464
00:56:56.370 --> 00:56:58.550
I barely row, I let everyone else win for me.

1465
00:56:58.550 --> 00:57:00.400
And that was when I started to understand this idea

1466
00:57:00.400 --> 00:57:03.040
that if everybody that you're

1467
00:57:03.040 --> 00:57:06.900
or the boat you're going into loves respects you

1468
00:57:06.900 --> 00:57:09.230
feels they owe you something

1469
00:57:09.230 --> 00:57:11.440
they will win the race for you.

1470
00:57:11.440 --> 00:57:12.300
And that's what he was doing.

1471
00:57:12.300 --> 00:57:15.281
He was the guy that would row extra mileage

1472
00:57:15.281 --> 00:57:18.330
at the end of practice cause someone needed some extra work.

1473
00:57:18.330 --> 00:57:20.350
Always be the guy spotting you at the gym,

1474
00:57:20.350 --> 00:57:22.707
picking you up from the bars if you were,

1475
00:57:22.707 --> 00:57:25.520
you're out a little late, not letting the coach know,

1476
00:57:25.520 --> 00:57:29.190
all this kinds of stuff, he was a players player.

1477
00:57:29.190 --> 00:57:32.190
He earned that trust and that loyalty over time.

1478
00:57:32.190 --> 00:57:34.170
And it was amazing.

1479
00:57:34.170 --> 00:57:38.670
And the, that was measurable out on the water.

1480
00:57:38.670 --> 00:57:41.930
That's an interesting thing that you can measure his loyalty

1481
00:57:41.930 --> 00:57:45.307
by the distance he would put between his boat

1482
00:57:45.307 --> 00:57:48.200
and the boat that was behind him that he just got out of.

1483
00:57:48.200 --> 00:57:51.600
And that was my first little like insight into

1484
00:57:52.540 --> 00:57:54.230
what it meant to be a real leader.

1485
00:57:54.230 --> 00:57:55.170
Yeah.

1486
00:57:55.170 --> 00:57:59.750
So you're getting this, this master's in geology,

1487
00:57:59.750 --> 00:58:01.778
but really what you're doing is you're,

1488
00:58:01.778 --> 00:58:05.000
you're getting, you're getting, it's sorta like

1489
00:58:05.000 --> 00:58:07.530
I got a master's degree and everything I needed to know

1490
00:58:07.530 --> 00:58:09.220
about team building and leadership

1491
00:58:09.220 --> 00:58:10.670
while sitting on a rowboat.

1492
00:58:10.670 --> 00:58:11.503
Exactly.
Right.

1493
00:58:11.503 --> 00:58:12.430
That's exactly what I'm doing.

1494
00:58:12.430 --> 00:58:15.340
That's the title of your next book, right?

1495
00:58:15.340 --> 00:58:17.743
So you go, you go back to Vesper,

1496
00:58:18.640 --> 00:58:20.190
and what's the goal like the Olympics,

1497
00:58:20.190 --> 00:58:22.640
like what's, what's the, you know.

1498
00:58:22.640 --> 00:58:26.350
Yeah, I think everyone's goal going to a place like Vesper

1499
00:58:26.350 --> 00:58:29.420
as an elite is to, make the national team

1500
00:58:29.420 --> 00:58:31.022
or the Olympics or both.

1501
00:58:31.022 --> 00:58:35.060
And, and so that's, that's why you're there.

1502
00:58:35.060 --> 00:58:36.740
I mean if you're not there for that reason,

1503
00:58:36.740 --> 00:58:39.360
you're probably not gonna last very long.

1504
00:58:39.360 --> 00:58:40.806
But again, my second one--

1505
00:58:40.806 --> 00:58:41.639
Well other than that what is there,

1506
00:58:41.639 --> 00:58:43.840
because it's not like you're,

1507
00:58:43.840 --> 00:58:45.445
making any money doing this.

1508
00:58:45.445 --> 00:58:48.850
You're out of college and this is your life.

1509
00:58:48.850 --> 00:58:51.000
Yeah. I mean, that is what it is.

1510
00:58:51.000 --> 00:58:52.607
I think there's sometimes you'll see a guy

1511
00:58:52.607 --> 00:58:55.520
who come from a pretty prestigious school

1512
00:58:55.520 --> 00:58:56.950
and you'll think, well, this guy serious,

1513
00:58:56.950 --> 00:58:58.910
he's gonna be a force.

1514
00:58:58.910 --> 00:58:59.880
And then he's just not that good.

1515
00:58:59.880 --> 00:59:01.720
You can realize that his heart's not in it.

1516
00:59:01.720 --> 00:59:03.910
Maybe he just wants to continue to live out

1517
00:59:03.910 --> 00:59:05.650
the glory days of his collegiate rowing.

1518
00:59:05.650 --> 00:59:06.840
And he doesn't make it that long.

1519
00:59:06.840 --> 00:59:10.050
Coach will cut them fast because he just it's

1520
00:59:10.050 --> 00:59:11.850
the demand on your time and on your body

1521
00:59:11.850 --> 00:59:14.810
is just too much to not have something like

1522
00:59:14.810 --> 00:59:17.830
the Olympics or the national team in your sights.

1523
00:59:17.830 --> 00:59:18.840
So yeah, we're all there.

1524
00:59:18.840 --> 00:59:20.160
The guys that are on the year round team

1525
00:59:20.160 --> 00:59:21.640
are there for that reason.

1526
00:59:21.640 --> 00:59:23.410
And it's a, it's a brotherhood,

1527
00:59:23.410 --> 00:59:24.577
it's a fraternity at that point.

1528
00:59:24.577 --> 00:59:25.890
And we're living in these,

1529
00:59:25.890 --> 00:59:27.810
this old historic boathouse anyway,

1530
00:59:27.810 --> 00:59:29.030
and that's what we're practicing every day.

1531
00:59:29.030 --> 00:59:31.680
And, so it's, it's a fraternity.

1532
00:59:31.680 --> 00:59:34.973
And so what the end game is,

1533
00:59:34.973 --> 00:59:38.530
is that some of us will make it and some of us will not.

1534
00:59:38.530 --> 00:59:39.363
That's, what's kind of--

1535
00:59:39.363 --> 00:59:41.100
So how does this play out for you?

1536
00:59:41.100 --> 00:59:43.223
Well, I spent about three years there,

1537
00:59:44.220 --> 00:59:47.580
go from this obscure baseball player slash rower

1538
00:59:47.580 --> 00:59:50.590
who probably shouldn't even be there, moved my way up into

1539
00:59:50.590 --> 00:59:53.120
the second boat and finally make my way

1540
00:59:53.120 --> 00:59:54.550
into the first boat.

1541
00:59:54.550 --> 00:59:56.477
And for me to do that--

1542
00:59:56.477 --> 00:59:59.540
I mean that's some pretty incredible progression

1543
00:59:59.540 --> 01:00:02.290
for somebody who's still pretty new to the sport.

1544
01:00:02.290 --> 01:00:03.123
Yeah. Thank you.

1545
01:00:03.123 --> 01:00:04.810
A couple of years to go,

1546
01:00:04.810 --> 01:00:08.200
from a guy who it's almost like they were doing a favor

1547
01:00:08.200 --> 01:00:10.680
to let you kind of tag along

1548
01:00:10.680 --> 01:00:13.860
to being in the number one boat.

1549
01:00:13.860 --> 01:00:14.720
Yeah, I know.

1550
01:00:14.720 --> 01:00:16.840
That's, one of my prouder moments

1551
01:00:16.840 --> 01:00:21.130
is making that team and, I worked hard to get there

1552
01:00:21.130 --> 01:00:24.250
and I think, Michiel took a chance on me

1553
01:00:24.250 --> 01:00:26.900
because by accepting me on that team,

1554
01:00:26.900 --> 01:00:29.060
somebody else didn't get accepted.

1555
01:00:29.060 --> 01:00:30.740
And that person has probably been rowing

1556
01:00:30.740 --> 01:00:31.627
a lot longer than me.

1557
01:00:31.627 --> 01:00:33.977
Was probably a lot faster than me on the water,

1558
01:00:34.840 --> 01:00:36.660
but Michiel saw something and I mean,

1559
01:00:36.660 --> 01:00:39.800
I'm not gonna argue with that because I felt that

1560
01:00:39.800 --> 01:00:41.500
first time I did not feel I deserve to be there,

1561
01:00:41.500 --> 01:00:44.110
but the second summer I did because I learned so much.

1562
01:00:44.110 --> 01:00:46.050
And so I worked my way up over the last,

1563
01:00:46.050 --> 01:00:47.470
over the next two and a half years to,

1564
01:00:47.470 --> 01:00:49.500
to get into that top boat.

1565
01:00:49.500 --> 01:00:53.600
And I remember the frustration of my,

1566
01:00:53.600 --> 01:00:56.270
my last summer there trying to get into that first boat

1567
01:00:56.270 --> 01:00:59.560
and Michiel was the coach of the women's team there too.

1568
01:00:59.560 --> 01:01:01.490
And that year they had a,

1569
01:01:01.490 --> 01:01:03.270
a women's lightweight double that was going

1570
01:01:03.270 --> 01:01:06.010
to a world championships. So he was focusing on them a lot.

1571
01:01:06.010 --> 01:01:08.930
And so he had another one of the coaches,

1572
01:01:08.930 --> 01:01:11.840
collegiate coach running our day-to-day practices.

1573
01:01:11.840 --> 01:01:15.820
He'd pop in a few times a week and run the practices

1574
01:01:15.820 --> 01:01:18.850
but for the most part, we had to answer to this guy.

1575
01:01:18.850 --> 01:01:21.630
And I would be seat raced.

1576
01:01:21.630 --> 01:01:23.680
I'd be switched, I'd win every one of my seat races.

1577
01:01:23.680 --> 01:01:25.650
And I'd still, when the lineups came up the next morning,

1578
01:01:25.650 --> 01:01:26.913
I would not be in that top boat.

1579
01:01:26.913 --> 01:01:28.240
And I was just getting pissed.

1580
01:01:28.240 --> 01:01:30.430
And now I'm friends with these guys,

1581
01:01:30.430 --> 01:01:31.670
I'm close with everybody.

1582
01:01:31.670 --> 01:01:34.020
And at this particular summer, that's when the,

1583
01:01:34.020 --> 01:01:36.040
they infuse it with the summer kids.

1584
01:01:36.040 --> 01:01:37.810
These are kids that are still at universities,

1585
01:01:37.810 --> 01:01:39.207
Princeton's Harvard Yales.

1586
01:01:39.207 --> 01:01:41.310
And I'm beating this Princeton guy,

1587
01:01:41.310 --> 01:01:42.730
every time for this seat.

1588
01:01:42.730 --> 01:01:45.020
And every time when the line it comes in the next day,

1589
01:01:45.020 --> 01:01:46.523
I'm not in the boat and he is.

1590
01:01:47.460 --> 01:01:48.293
And had gotten close to Michiel

1591
01:01:48.293 --> 01:01:49.360
over the last couple of years,

1592
01:01:49.360 --> 01:01:52.060
but I was afraid to ask him, but I called on a favor.

1593
01:01:52.060 --> 01:01:55.390
I went into his office. I said, I'm frustrated.

1594
01:01:55.390 --> 01:01:57.990
I deserve to be in that top boat, I'm faster.

1595
01:01:57.990 --> 01:02:00.900
The team knows on faster, the year round guys all think,

1596
01:02:00.900 --> 01:02:02.760
they say you should be in that boat, Jay.

1597
01:02:02.760 --> 01:02:05.060
And, but I'm not in it. I'm winning.

1598
01:02:05.060 --> 01:02:08.090
And I said, I just like to see if

1599
01:02:08.090 --> 01:02:09.650
I'm not asking for you to put me in,

1600
01:02:09.650 --> 01:02:12.640
I'm asking for you to watch a seat race.

1601
01:02:12.640 --> 01:02:15.717
And I remember him saying, he says, I'm busy. We'll see.

1602
01:02:15.717 --> 01:02:18.720
And it was, it wasn't really the response I was looking for.

1603
01:02:18.720 --> 01:02:21.510
So I was like okay, that made me not, then I felt bad,

1604
01:02:21.510 --> 01:02:23.710
but the next morning he's there.

1605
01:02:23.710 --> 01:02:25.730
And he comes to the morning workout.

1606
01:02:25.730 --> 01:02:28.110
He says, all right, I'm gonna be running seat racing.

1607
01:02:28.110 --> 01:02:30.500
And he says, so let's get out the boats.

1608
01:02:30.500 --> 01:02:31.720
Here's the lineups we're going.

1609
01:02:31.720 --> 01:02:33.017
So he's in the launch with the coach

1610
01:02:33.017 --> 01:02:35.270
who was coaching us that summer.

1611
01:02:35.270 --> 01:02:36.560
And we go out there and you never know

1612
01:02:36.560 --> 01:02:39.900
who's gonna get picked, but my name gets called.

1613
01:02:39.900 --> 01:02:42.920
We row, we get switched. I win.

1614
01:02:42.920 --> 01:02:44.930
And he just pulls the, his little bull horn out,

1615
01:02:44.930 --> 01:02:47.810
says from now on Jason's in the top boat, he won it.

1616
01:02:47.810 --> 01:02:51.330
And I was probably one of the best days of my rowing career.

1617
01:02:51.330 --> 01:02:53.470
And I went back to him and I just said, I appreciate that.

1618
01:02:53.470 --> 01:02:54.920
That you took the time.

1619
01:02:54.920 --> 01:02:56.710
He said, I knew I was gonna do it, but I couldn't tell you,

1620
01:02:56.710 --> 01:02:57.850
cause I didn't wanna give you

1621
01:02:57.850 --> 01:02:59.800
an advantage over your teammate

1622
01:02:59.800 --> 01:03:02.660
when you knew you were gonna seat race that day.

1623
01:03:02.660 --> 01:03:03.785
And he said, you deserved it.

1624
01:03:03.785 --> 01:03:05.730
And and I was able to make it there.

1625
01:03:05.730 --> 01:03:09.930
But that was largely because the team that I had spent

1626
01:03:09.930 --> 01:03:13.377
the last two years with day in and day out had,

1627
01:03:13.377 --> 01:03:16.330
I'd grown to love, they'd grown to love me.

1628
01:03:16.330 --> 01:03:18.180
And there was, there was a palpable feeling

1629
01:03:18.180 --> 01:03:19.300
when I got moved into the boat

1630
01:03:19.300 --> 01:03:22.180
and those guys look behind me, let's go J right now.

1631
01:03:22.180 --> 01:03:24.473
So then how far do you end up taking this?

1632
01:03:25.390 --> 01:03:26.330
I take it,

1633
01:03:26.330 --> 01:03:28.550
Win, a couple of gold medals in nationals,

1634
01:03:28.550 --> 01:03:30.960
a silver and a bronze, these kinds of things doing well,

1635
01:03:30.960 --> 01:03:32.210
but didn't qualify

1636
01:03:32.210 --> 01:03:36.780
for Beijing Olympic trials for 2008.

1637
01:03:36.780 --> 01:03:38.097
I mean, some guys did on our team.

1638
01:03:38.097 --> 01:03:40.860
And I just, wasn't one of those guys.

1639
01:03:40.860 --> 01:03:42.240
How does the selection process work

1640
01:03:42.240 --> 01:03:43.243
for the Olympics and rowing?

1641
01:03:43.243 --> 01:03:46.230
There's two ways to get selected in rowing.

1642
01:03:46.230 --> 01:03:48.020
One is to make the national team.

1643
01:03:48.020 --> 01:03:49.780
There's certain boats and the Olympics

1644
01:03:49.780 --> 01:03:53.420
where the national team coach simply selects the crew

1645
01:03:53.420 --> 01:03:54.540
that's gonna be in that boat.

1646
01:03:54.540 --> 01:03:56.165
So it's called the selection boat.

1647
01:03:56.165 --> 01:03:58.720
So you have to be on the national team.

1648
01:03:58.720 --> 01:04:01.560
And like the men's eight, for instance, is a selection boat,

1649
01:04:01.560 --> 01:04:04.490
or at least it was when I was there. I still think it is.

1650
01:04:04.490 --> 01:04:07.330
In which case the national team coach will say, all right,

1651
01:04:07.330 --> 01:04:09.380
these are the guys that are going to the Olympics.

1652
01:04:09.380 --> 01:04:11.370
Yeah. That's gotta be controversial.

1653
01:04:11.370 --> 01:04:12.343
Because that's highly subjective. Right?

1654
01:04:12.343 --> 01:04:14.670
I think a lot of people wish that they were,

1655
01:04:14.670 --> 01:04:15.810
it was all the other way,

1656
01:04:15.810 --> 01:04:17.570
which is what's called the trials boat.

1657
01:04:17.570 --> 01:04:19.310
So the rest of the, the other boats

1658
01:04:19.310 --> 01:04:21.280
that aren't selection boats are trials.

1659
01:04:21.280 --> 01:04:24.610
It's an, it's like the U.S. open. Anybody can enter.

1660
01:04:24.610 --> 01:04:27.640
You go to the open trials and you race.

1661
01:04:27.640 --> 01:04:30.990
And if your team wins, your crew wins, you go.

1662
01:04:30.990 --> 01:04:35.750
And at that point, I was in my best chance was,

1663
01:04:35.750 --> 01:04:37.490
was in a mens double.

1664
01:04:37.490 --> 01:04:42.070
So myself and my teammate, Mike Ross.

1665
01:04:42.070 --> 01:04:44.480
And we rode all pretty much all summer together

1666
01:04:44.480 --> 01:04:47.360
to try to try to, to try to be able to qualify.

1667
01:04:47.360 --> 01:04:49.130
But we, we didn't qualify.

1668
01:04:49.130 --> 01:04:53.255
We were, it was gonna be a long shot at best anyway for us.

1669
01:04:53.255 --> 01:04:55.550
Mike's a very good rower,

1670
01:04:55.550 --> 01:04:58.300
came from Marietta, very good rowing school,

1671
01:04:58.300 --> 01:05:00.053
incredibly gifted rower.

1672
01:05:01.300 --> 01:05:05.070
But, and we were, we were so hot, hot and cold all the time.

1673
01:05:05.070 --> 01:05:07.090
Sometimes we'd win a race, by a landslide,

1674
01:05:07.090 --> 01:05:10.150
and other times we wouldn't even get out of the heats so.

1675
01:05:10.150 --> 01:05:11.390
We just weren't able to qualify.

1676
01:05:11.390 --> 01:05:13.186
And it's still like, to this day, I just,

1677
01:05:13.186 --> 01:05:15.830
I think I just didn't have enough time, I'm still,

1678
01:05:15.830 --> 01:05:18.690
this is my third year of rowing at this point.

1679
01:05:18.690 --> 01:05:21.250
I mean, some people I had the last Olympics, I wasn't,

1680
01:05:21.250 --> 01:05:22.510
didn't even know what rowing was

1681
01:05:22.510 --> 01:05:24.660
when people were in the last Olympics so.

1682
01:05:24.660 --> 01:05:27.470
Right, so you get that far,

1683
01:05:27.470 --> 01:05:28.700
but are you thinking, all right,

1684
01:05:28.700 --> 01:05:32.470
I'm in it for the next four years for 2012 or,

1685
01:05:32.470 --> 01:05:33.850
you're gonna stick it out for that,

1686
01:05:33.850 --> 01:05:35.240
or how does that play out?

1687
01:05:35.240 --> 01:05:36.830
Yeah, that was the crossroads.

1688
01:05:36.830 --> 01:05:39.772
And I'm, trying to make that decision

1689
01:05:39.772 --> 01:05:44.110
and it's just, I put so much into it

1690
01:05:44.110 --> 01:05:46.310
and not that I expected to go.

1691
01:05:46.310 --> 01:05:48.770
I didn't, I was a long shot the entire time.

1692
01:05:48.770 --> 01:05:51.400
Like it just, I was small.

1693
01:05:51.400 --> 01:05:53.320
I was inexperienced compared to these guys,

1694
01:05:53.320 --> 01:05:56.370
but the thought of being another four years,

1695
01:05:56.370 --> 01:05:59.348
training and Philly putting off my career,

1696
01:05:59.348 --> 01:06:02.130
it was just, it seemed too much.

1697
01:06:02.130 --> 01:06:05.560
And so I'm not, not sure if like 100%

1698
01:06:05.560 --> 01:06:08.220
I made the right decision, but my decision was to be done.

1699
01:06:08.220 --> 01:06:09.053
So I retired.

1700
01:06:09.053 --> 01:06:10.850
Right so with that,

1701
01:06:10.850 --> 01:06:12.640
it kind of goes back to how I opened this,

1702
01:06:12.640 --> 01:06:16.260
which is you're this guy who has a lot of athletic talent,

1703
01:06:16.260 --> 01:06:19.660
you've got a lot of drive, you've demonstrated,

1704
01:06:19.660 --> 01:06:22.350
extreme potential you've had some success

1705
01:06:22.350 --> 01:06:25.990
but there's this sense of unfinished business, right?

1706
01:06:25.990 --> 01:06:28.070
Like, so you can go become a civilian,

1707
01:06:28.070 --> 01:06:31.450
but you're walking around sort of like a ticking time bomb

1708
01:06:31.450 --> 01:06:33.770
because there's this thing inside of you

1709
01:06:33.770 --> 01:06:36.213
that is yearning to come out.

1710
01:06:37.190 --> 01:06:41.990
Yeah. And it's just, I move back to California,

1711
01:06:41.990 --> 01:06:43.053
and that's what it is.

1712
01:06:43.053 --> 01:06:45.830
It's a restlessness begins to kind of set in.

1713
01:06:45.830 --> 01:06:48.980
And this permanent restlessness is just a hard thing

1714
01:06:48.980 --> 01:06:49.813
to cope with.

1715
01:06:49.813 --> 01:06:52.863
And I remember I'm not home for more than, I don't know,

1716
01:06:52.863 --> 01:06:55.520
four or five months then I decide to go travel.

1717
01:06:55.520 --> 01:06:59.180
So I leave on pretty much traveling off and on

1718
01:06:59.180 --> 01:07:00.013
for another year,

1719
01:07:00.013 --> 01:07:01.410
going to Europe and all these other places.

1720
01:07:01.410 --> 01:07:04.310
And, I just, I'm trying to find that thing.

1721
01:07:04.310 --> 01:07:08.710
And in the end, it was,

1722
01:07:08.710 --> 01:07:10.250
I was trying to just settle down permanently,

1723
01:07:10.250 --> 01:07:12.250
but I think it was just an intermission.

1724
01:07:13.570 --> 01:07:14.510 line:15%
Was there an aware, like,

1725
01:07:14.510 --> 01:07:16.870 line:15%
what is the relationship between

1726
01:07:16.870 --> 01:07:21.340 line:15%
like the hardcore national team, Olympic caliber rowers,

1727
01:07:21.340 --> 01:07:24.270
and this whole other culture of ocean rowing.

1728
01:07:24.270 --> 01:07:26.140
Cause those, yes.

1729
01:07:26.140 --> 01:07:29.250
One's gonna feed the other, but they don't really intersect.

1730
01:07:29.250 --> 01:07:31.050
I mean, they're very different disciplines, right.

1731
01:07:31.050 --> 01:07:34.440
So is it looked down upon, is it revered?

1732
01:07:34.440 --> 01:07:36.440
Like, how does that interplay

1733
01:07:36.440 --> 01:07:38.710
between those two cultures work?

1734
01:07:38.710 --> 01:07:40.600
Well, I think they're actually just now starting to

1735
01:07:40.600 --> 01:07:43.400
blend in more with open ocean rowing

1736
01:07:43.400 --> 01:07:45.540
coming into the Olympics and becoming more

1737
01:07:45.540 --> 01:07:46.860
of an international sport.

1738
01:07:46.860 --> 01:07:47.720
So before--

1739
01:07:47.720 --> 01:07:50.427
Is open, open ocean rowing in the Olympics?

1740
01:07:50.427 --> 01:07:51.957
Its going to become an Olympic sport,

1741
01:07:51.957 --> 01:07:53.940
not trans ocean rowing,

1742
01:07:53.940 --> 01:07:56.330
but open ocean rowing where you're going distances

1743
01:07:56.330 --> 01:07:57.750
in open ocean.

1744
01:07:57.750 --> 01:07:59.177
And that's exciting for the sport.

1745
01:07:59.177 --> 01:08:02.410
And that is now starting, you're starting to see a blend.

1746
01:08:02.410 --> 01:08:05.000
So you now you're starting to see flat water rowers

1747
01:08:05.000 --> 01:08:07.900
or sprint rowers, collegiate and elite level rowers

1748
01:08:07.900 --> 01:08:11.730
start to think about transitioning in to open ocean.

1749
01:08:11.730 --> 01:08:15.330
When I started open ocean rowing, there, there was,

1750
01:08:15.330 --> 01:08:17.100
there was really no, we were the overlap.

1751
01:08:17.100 --> 01:08:18.730
But that was the big idea, right?

1752
01:08:18.730 --> 01:08:20.530
Like here, I'm gonna take everything I know

1753
01:08:20.530 --> 01:08:23.890
about athleticism and apply it to this world

1754
01:08:23.890 --> 01:08:27.820
that needs, an upgrade, like we're gonna approach this

1755
01:08:27.820 --> 01:08:30.650
from an elite athlete perspective,

1756
01:08:30.650 --> 01:08:35.420
versus like a salty sea where the, rogue kind of,

1757
01:08:35.420 --> 01:08:38.540
veteran ocean row guy perspective.

1758
01:08:38.540 --> 01:08:39.373
Exactly.

1759
01:08:39.373 --> 01:08:41.110
And so I would say at the time that I was starting

1760
01:08:41.110 --> 01:08:44.050
to do ocean rowing and doing exactly what you said,

1761
01:08:44.050 --> 01:08:47.080
