without actually saying, okay guys,

2176
01:24:02.330 --> 01:24:03.450
we're gonna do this breakfast thing.

2177
01:24:03.450 --> 01:24:06.330
Cause you can't, it has to be organic.

2178
01:24:06.330 --> 01:24:07.853
And I'm just realizing that,

2179
01:24:09.220 --> 01:24:11.090
as nice as it was to have one other guy

2180
01:24:11.090 --> 01:24:13.990
that you were kind of leveraging as human emotion,

2181
01:24:13.990 --> 01:24:15.640
he in turn was doing that to you.

2182
01:24:17.117 --> 01:24:19.530
Like, wouldn't it be nice if it was for the whole boat?

2183
01:24:19.530 --> 01:24:21.920
And then I like, I really didn't stop there.

2184
01:24:21.920 --> 01:24:22.753
I was thinking like, well,

2185
01:24:22.753 --> 01:24:23.882
we should include our family members.

2186
01:24:23.882 --> 01:24:26.780
like our wives and girlfriends and

2187
01:24:26.780 --> 01:24:29.640
our extended family and our friends and our neighbors.

2188
01:24:29.640 --> 01:24:32.540
I was like, if we created a community of people

2189
01:24:32.540 --> 01:24:36.320
that once we got to the boat and we started rowing,

2190
01:24:36.320 --> 01:24:39.423
we felt we're relying on us,

2191
01:24:40.310 --> 01:24:42.043
then that would be a strong motivator and then.

2192
01:24:42.043 --> 01:24:43.460
And so that's what I did.

2193
01:24:43.460 --> 01:24:45.330
I just basically started to create a community.

2194
01:24:45.330 --> 01:24:48.120
So we rowed and we got bigger and stronger

2195
01:24:48.120 --> 01:24:51.000
and we rowed together, but I, we went out there,

2196
01:24:51.000 --> 01:24:53.600
they came out our way and we just spent a lot of time

2197
01:24:53.600 --> 01:24:54.433
together as a team.

2198
01:24:54.433 --> 01:24:57.240
And it was, it was rowing, but it was also dinners.

2199
01:24:57.240 --> 01:24:58.300
It was drinks.

2200
01:24:58.300 --> 01:25:00.320
It was meeting each other's wives and girlfriends

2201
01:25:00.320 --> 01:25:01.720
and spending time with them.

2202
01:25:02.730 --> 01:25:06.360
It was, it was, it was brotherhood.

2203
01:25:06.360 --> 01:25:08.560
It was fraternity at that point, building this community,

2204
01:25:08.560 --> 01:25:09.790
this sense of community.

2205
01:25:09.790 --> 01:25:13.620
And then it was, if anybody in our family group,

2206
01:25:13.620 --> 01:25:16.570
our friend group, our neighbors wanted a job on this team

2207
01:25:16.570 --> 01:25:17.720
they wanted to be part of this team

2208
01:25:17.720 --> 01:25:18.912
we'd find something for them to do.

2209
01:25:18.912 --> 01:25:21.290
Something that we needed done so that when they did it,

2210
01:25:21.290 --> 01:25:23.423
they felt they were part of the narrative.

2211
01:25:24.270 --> 01:25:27.020
So you go fast forward to we're at the start line with,

2212
01:25:28.300 --> 01:25:30.990
or back at La Gomera, the start line a year later.

2213
01:25:30.990 --> 01:25:33.060
And there's, it feels different.

2214
01:25:33.060 --> 01:25:36.020
It feels like there is a lot more riding on this.

2215
01:25:36.020 --> 01:25:37.640
And when I go back to that thing,

2216
01:25:37.640 --> 01:25:38.910
where glory gets you to the start line,

2217
01:25:38.910 --> 01:25:40.270
but shame gets you through the finish.

2218
01:25:40.270 --> 01:25:42.230
There was this idea of feeling that we,

2219
01:25:42.230 --> 01:25:44.490
we had a lot of people that were,

2220
01:25:44.490 --> 01:25:47.220
that were looking up to us to do this,

2221
01:25:47.220 --> 01:25:50.240
to finish the narrative that they were a part of.

2222
01:25:50.240 --> 01:25:55.240
And that was a strong motivator and something again,

2223
01:25:56.160 --> 01:25:59.020
that I was kind of learning in its entirety

2224
01:25:59.020 --> 01:26:00.880
for the first time that

2225
01:26:01.730 --> 01:26:05.003
you've got people that are not saying,

2226
01:26:06.290 --> 01:26:09.560
do this or else, but are saying that I'm part of this story,

2227
01:26:09.560 --> 01:26:10.940
I'm passing the baton to you now,

2228
01:26:10.940 --> 01:26:12.940
now it's up to you guys, finish this story,

2229
01:26:12.940 --> 01:26:14.490
finish the unfinished business.

2230
01:26:16.380 --> 01:26:17.863
The stakes were a lot higher. Yeah.

2231
01:26:17.863 --> 01:26:19.270
Yeah.

2232
01:26:19.270 --> 01:26:22.860
It's not just about you in some kind of vainglorious thing.

2233
01:26:22.860 --> 01:26:25.980
You've got a lot of people invested in this,

2234
01:26:25.980 --> 01:26:27.310
but for the right reasons.

2235
01:26:27.310 --> 01:26:30.509
I'm sure everybody has their distinct respective why,

2236
01:26:30.509 --> 01:26:33.280
your job as a leader is to figure out

2237
01:26:33.280 --> 01:26:35.380
how to take all of those whys and,

2238
01:26:35.380 --> 01:26:38.260
and have them cohere to one kind of singular why

2239
01:26:38.260 --> 01:26:41.430
that will unite your yourself as a team

2240
01:26:41.430 --> 01:26:43.250
so that when you meet those obstacles,

2241
01:26:43.250 --> 01:26:46.660
which are a certainty and are gonna come in many

2242
01:26:46.660 --> 01:26:50.230
unpredictable ways that you'll be able to tap into

2243
01:26:50.230 --> 01:26:52.690
a different gear,
Exactly.

2244
01:26:52.690 --> 01:26:54.310
To support each other through it.

2245
01:26:54.310 --> 01:26:58.010
Yeah, I think that one of the many leadership burdens

2246
01:26:58.010 --> 01:27:02.390
that you have as a captain is that you need to take

2247
01:27:02.390 --> 01:27:05.320
everyone's individual-wise and somehow convince them

2248
01:27:05.320 --> 01:27:08.070
that by going through with the team why

2249
01:27:08.070 --> 01:27:10.000
that they will still be able to achieve

2250
01:27:10.000 --> 01:27:12.540
their individual wHYs. And that's tough thing to do.

2251
01:27:12.540 --> 01:27:14.230
Look, Matt was doing it for a certain reason.

2252
01:27:14.230 --> 01:27:16.550
Alex was doing it for another reason, Angus and myself,

2253
01:27:16.550 --> 01:27:18.950
we all had different reasons why we were there.

2254
01:27:18.950 --> 01:27:23.950
But what I try to convince them all is that to go

2255
01:27:25.070 --> 01:27:28.410
as fast as we can for as long as we can to go as hard

2256
01:27:28.410 --> 01:27:30.560
to give everything that we have out on that water,

2257
01:27:30.560 --> 01:27:34.180
will simultaneously take care of all of our other

2258
01:27:34.180 --> 01:27:36.170
singular agenda objectives.

2259
01:27:36.170 --> 01:27:40.010
And I think that's where that trust piece comes in

2260
01:27:40.010 --> 01:27:42.810
as a leader, is that people have to trust that

2261
01:27:42.810 --> 01:27:46.210
their individual objectives are gonna be taken care of

2262
01:27:46.210 --> 01:27:48.463
if they buy into the team objective.

2263
01:27:49.300 --> 01:27:52.630
That is a difficult thing to convince people.

2264
01:27:52.630 --> 01:27:54.530
Yeah, I would think so.

2265
01:27:54.530 --> 01:27:58.160
And even probably more difficult in a corporate context.

2266
01:27:58.160 --> 01:28:00.240
I mean, it's, the principles are the same, right?

2267
01:28:00.240 --> 01:28:03.120
Whether you're rowing a boat or you're leading a team

2268
01:28:03.120 --> 01:28:05.890
at some corporation as the leader,

2269
01:28:05.890 --> 01:28:07.740
you have to get everybody to,

2270
01:28:07.740 --> 01:28:09.980
to buy into some kind of singular vision.

2271
01:28:09.980 --> 01:28:13.310
And you're asking these people to give of themselves.

2272
01:28:13.310 --> 01:28:15.290
So what is their why for doing that?

2273
01:28:15.290 --> 01:28:17.060
And why should they trust you?

2274
01:28:17.060 --> 01:28:19.930
And when things get difficult, how are they gonna,

2275
01:28:19.930 --> 01:28:21.930
kind of stick with you and not just bail

2276
01:28:21.930 --> 01:28:24.030
and go get a job somewhere else.

2277
01:28:24.030 --> 01:28:25.000
Yeah, absolutely.

2278
01:28:25.000 --> 01:28:28.480
And I think in a lot of times, and as part of my job,

2279
01:28:28.480 --> 01:28:31.130
I work with these corporations and teach this leadership.

2280
01:28:31.130 --> 01:28:33.825
And one of the things that they say is is that your,

2281
01:28:33.825 --> 01:28:35.920
your stories are great and your examples are great,

2282
01:28:35.920 --> 01:28:37.850
but like, I'm not rowing across an ocean

2283
01:28:37.850 --> 01:28:41.130
I'm just, I'm just doing this one single thing.

2284
01:28:41.130 --> 01:28:44.100
I think when you're in a corporation,

2285
01:28:44.100 --> 01:28:45.230
these corporate leaders,

2286
01:28:45.230 --> 01:28:48.380
their burden is to enoble, or like to make noble,

2287
01:28:48.380 --> 01:28:50.940
the effort that they are putting forth.

2288
01:28:50.940 --> 01:28:55.860
And when you're asking, and this is something that I'm,

2289
01:28:55.860 --> 01:28:59.250
this is something I'm quite passionate about is that

2290
01:28:59.250 --> 01:29:02.200
if you're asking someone to give all of themselves

2291
01:29:03.202 --> 01:29:04.950
to something, then you are also asking them

2292
01:29:04.950 --> 01:29:06.620
to be changed by that something.

2293
01:29:06.620 --> 01:29:07.453
I mean, that's what happens.

2294
01:29:07.453 --> 01:29:08.417
That's what happened with Tom and I,

2295
01:29:08.417 --> 01:29:10.080
and this is what I'm understanding is

2296
01:29:10.080 --> 01:29:13.590
we're in that second row is Tom and I are different people.

2297
01:29:13.590 --> 01:29:16.150
We're not the same people that left the shores

2298
01:29:16.150 --> 01:29:17.660
of La Gomera at the start line,

2299
01:29:17.660 --> 01:29:19.980
which is different because we gave all of ourselves to

2300
01:29:19.980 --> 01:29:21.557
this endeavor and to each other, we're just different now.

2301
01:29:21.557 --> 01:29:24.040
And we talk about it all the time.

2302
01:29:24.040 --> 01:29:26.060
And I'm also different

2303
01:29:26.060 --> 01:29:28.340
than I was before I left for that second row,

2304
01:29:28.340 --> 01:29:29.173
because I gave all myself.

2305
01:29:29.173 --> 01:29:31.660
And so did the team, we're all just different people now.

2306
01:29:31.660 --> 01:29:35.300
But that's not a casual thing to be asking someone to do

2307
01:29:35.300 --> 01:29:36.870
because there's a lot of people out there that say look,

2308
01:29:36.870 --> 01:29:39.990
I just wanna come into work every day and do

2309
01:29:39.990 --> 01:29:43.960
a really good job and then leave and then forget about it

2310
01:29:43.960 --> 01:29:45.580
and then come back and do the same thing the next day.

2311
01:29:45.580 --> 01:29:48.450
And that's fine, by the way, like that's absolutely fine.

2312
01:29:48.450 --> 01:29:49.769
But like, if you're a part of a,

2313
01:29:49.769 --> 01:29:52.880
if you're trying to like create, like you said,

2314
01:29:52.880 --> 01:29:56.930
like this idea of high-performance and yeah,

2315
01:29:56.930 --> 01:29:58.690
there's a lot of parallels between it.

2316
01:29:58.690 --> 01:29:59.950
Then that's what you're asking people to do.

2317
01:29:59.950 --> 01:30:02.120
You're asking people to actually be changed by,

2318
01:30:02.120 --> 01:30:03.550
I mean that's why I love change. That's right.

2319
01:30:03.550 --> 01:30:05.790
We give ourselves all of ourselves to somebody

2320
01:30:05.790 --> 01:30:08.250
and now we're different people because of it, we're in love.

2321
01:30:08.250 --> 01:30:12.220
And you must first be able to answer that for yourself.

2322
01:30:12.220 --> 01:30:13.800
And be okay with that with yourself.

2323
01:30:13.800 --> 01:30:15.890
Am I okay with being changed by the thing we're about to do?

2324
01:30:15.890 --> 01:30:18.720
Am I okay with being a different person when I come home.

2325
01:30:18.720 --> 01:30:20.130
Then once you've done that,

2326
01:30:20.130 --> 01:30:22.650
you need to be able to do that with everybody else.

2327
01:30:22.650 --> 01:30:23.960
People are gonna resist that.

2328
01:30:23.960 --> 01:30:27.020
Yeah. People are messy.
People are messy.

2329
01:30:27.020 --> 01:30:30.760
And that differentiator between just,

2330
01:30:30.760 --> 01:30:33.560
showing up and going through a certain routine

2331
01:30:33.560 --> 01:30:36.320
versus the high performance version of that

2332
01:30:36.320 --> 01:30:39.430
really boils down to that emotional piece,

2333
01:30:39.430 --> 01:30:41.750
understanding what motivates people,

2334
01:30:41.750 --> 01:30:43.940
how they tick, finding a way to tap into that

2335
01:30:43.940 --> 01:30:46.030
and engender that level of trust,

2336
01:30:46.030 --> 01:30:50.100
and then doing that kind of unromantic community building,

2337
01:30:50.100 --> 01:30:50.933
right?

2338
01:30:50.933 --> 01:30:52.910
Like that's, and that's what you did that second time

2339
01:30:52.910 --> 01:30:53.743
building that team.

2340
01:30:53.743 --> 01:30:56.610
Like, how do we, how do we create a community

2341
01:30:56.610 --> 01:30:59.910
that is gonna be self-supporting in all the best ways

2342
01:30:59.910 --> 01:31:01.540
while you have to put in the time,

2343
01:31:01.540 --> 01:31:04.100
you have to invest the energy into doing that.

2344
01:31:04.100 --> 01:31:05.800
Yeah. And that's the, like you said,

2345
01:31:05.800 --> 01:31:08.730
that's the non-sexy stuff that would, that the day to day.

2346
01:31:08.730 --> 01:31:13.410
99% of all this stuff is not, great.

2347
01:31:13.410 --> 01:31:14.760
I mean, think about the row.

2348
01:31:14.760 --> 01:31:15.980
Like the start is great.

2349
01:31:15.980 --> 01:31:17.191
Cause everyone's taking pictures, you're pushing off--

2350
01:31:17.191 --> 01:31:19.390
Why can't everyone just be wherever they live,

2351
01:31:19.390 --> 01:31:21.820
train really hard show up and we'll just crush this.

2352
01:31:21.820 --> 01:31:24.290
Yeah, exactly. That make my life a lot easier.

2353
01:31:24.290 --> 01:31:25.123
Yeah right.

2354
01:31:25.123 --> 01:31:26.230
Still trying to figure out what that is,

2355
01:31:26.230 --> 01:31:27.793
but that's what you got.

2356
01:31:27.793 --> 01:31:30.840
I mean, the start is great and the finish is great

2357
01:31:30.840 --> 01:31:35.090
and the middle is brutal and ruthless and uncomfortable,

2358
01:31:35.090 --> 01:31:36.390
and you wanna quit,

2359
01:31:36.390 --> 01:31:38.798
and you're getting into arguments with your teammates,

2360
01:31:38.798 --> 01:31:41.540
and stress levels high, and danger is high.

2361
01:31:41.540 --> 01:31:45.410
And you're rowing a million and a half strokes give or take.

2362
01:31:45.410 --> 01:31:49.130
I mean, there's a lot of mundaness in that,

2363
01:31:49.130 --> 01:31:52.050
not unlike the corporate world where you're saying,

2364
01:31:52.050 --> 01:31:52.883
oh, this is all great,

2365
01:31:52.883 --> 01:31:54.250
but every day I just do this stuff.

2366
01:31:54.250 --> 01:31:57.120
Like what if what, if I'm in procurement,

2367
01:31:57.120 --> 01:32:00.430
why does my job matter to larger organization?

2368
01:32:00.430 --> 01:32:01.960
The larger picture.

2369
01:32:01.960 --> 01:32:04.920
The leader needs to prove that it does prove that it does.

2370
01:32:04.920 --> 01:32:06.730
That consistency piece.

2371
01:32:06.730 --> 01:32:09.130
My favorite part of the documentary is,

2372
01:32:09.130 --> 01:32:12.880
when you guys are pushing off for this second Atlantic row.

2373
01:32:12.880 --> 01:32:16.040
And, it's Matt, right?

2374
01:32:16.040 --> 01:32:17.450
He just, he just hits it hard.

2375
01:32:17.450 --> 01:32:18.790
Like he's just going for it.

2376
01:32:18.790 --> 01:32:22.420
Like in that first couple hours, you're like, bro,

2377
01:32:22.420 --> 01:32:25.640
we're gonna be out here for like, a month.

2378
01:32:25.640 --> 01:32:26.970
Oh man.
Chill out.

2379
01:32:26.970 --> 01:32:29.360
But it's that competitive? Like that's what he knows.

2380
01:32:29.360 --> 01:32:30.193
He's yeah, no experience.

2381
01:32:30.193 --> 01:32:33.780
And he was the guy who didn't have the ocean experience

2382
01:32:33.780 --> 01:32:37.140
to realize like, hey man, you gotta like take it breezy.

2383
01:32:37.140 --> 01:32:38.547
Yeah. He was taking it like a 2000 unit.

2384
01:32:38.547 --> 01:32:41.620
And he just face-planted for like a week.

2385
01:32:41.620 --> 01:32:44.800
And I'm having deja vu with Nick the year before.

2386
01:32:44.800 --> 01:32:48.750
I mean, he goes in there, he goes in so hard and as he says,

2387
01:32:48.750 --> 01:32:51.390
I was highly motivated. I'm like yeah, I know you were.

2388
01:32:51.390 --> 01:32:52.450
Yeah. But you can't sustain--

2389
01:32:52.450 --> 01:32:53.880
In the most unsustainable way.

2390
01:32:53.880 --> 01:32:54.713
Yeah, exactly.

2391
01:32:54.713 --> 01:32:56.760
I mean, we're out here for over a month and he's going out.

2392
01:32:56.760 --> 01:32:59.070
I mean, he's drenched in sweat in the first three hours.

2393
01:32:59.070 --> 01:33:03.970
I mean, he's eating this like pasta dish that just I'm,

2394
01:33:03.970 --> 01:33:06.270
I'm sitting here going, I eating a dry granola bar.

2395
01:33:06.270 --> 01:33:07.780
I'm just, I'm waiting for it to hit me

2396
01:33:07.780 --> 01:33:09.640
because the seasickness is gonna hit.

2397
01:33:09.640 --> 01:33:13.080
And it hits him and man, it knocks him flat on the ground

2398
01:33:13.080 --> 01:33:16.610
and he is out five or six days.

2399
01:33:16.610 --> 01:33:18.670
And I'm in, I'm having deja vu from Nick

2400
01:33:18.670 --> 01:33:20.210
because he's doing the exact same thing.

2401
01:33:20.210 --> 01:33:22.950
He's in the cabin and I'll never forget,

2402
01:33:22.950 --> 01:33:24.127
we're taking shifts off of him.

2403
01:33:24.127 --> 01:33:25.650
And this is where,

2404
01:33:25.650 --> 01:33:28.310
I've got a chance to change the narrative here.

2405
01:33:28.310 --> 01:33:30.480
And I just, I just remember we,

2406
01:33:30.480 --> 01:33:34.500
we took shifts from him and, we kept going in there and,

2407
01:33:34.500 --> 01:33:36.560
he was just lying face down.

2408
01:33:36.560 --> 01:33:39.200
And I remember we had taken shifts I was like,

2409
01:33:39.200 --> 01:33:40.610
I got to at least see if you can row.

2410
01:33:40.610 --> 01:33:42.417
And I'll just never forget. He just looks up.

2411
01:33:42.417 --> 01:33:43.540
And I said, can you do it?

2412
01:33:43.540 --> 01:33:46.480
And he just looks up and says, we'll see.

2413
01:33:46.480 --> 01:33:48.263
And it was just like, I mean, this is,

2414
01:33:48.263 --> 01:33:53.210
this is a guy who gets into UCLA on academic merit,

2415
01:33:53.210 --> 01:33:56.180
finds out. He can row gets, gets recruited by Yale

2416
01:33:56.180 --> 01:33:58.270
after his first year then goes to Oxford

2417
01:33:58.270 --> 01:33:59.500
as if it's not good enough for him.

2418
01:33:59.500 --> 01:34:00.350
I mean, this guy just is--

2419
01:34:00.350 --> 01:34:02.130
And it sounds like he came from kind of

2420
01:34:02.130 --> 01:34:03.410
a messed up childhood, right?

2421
01:34:03.410 --> 01:34:05.320
Like it was a pretty rough situation.

2422
01:34:05.320 --> 01:34:07.590
He had a rough situation, that's his story, not mine,

2423
01:34:07.590 --> 01:34:11.423
but he, he had it bad and he,

2424
01:34:12.610 --> 01:34:15.330
he is, as he says,

2425
01:34:15.330 --> 01:34:18.120
I looked at teammates as obstacles that I had to crush.

2426
01:34:18.120 --> 01:34:19.060
And I always just thought that was such

2427
01:34:19.060 --> 01:34:20.620
an interesting way of looking at,

2428
01:34:20.620 --> 01:34:24.880
that's his whole life was that the people that I was with

2429
01:34:24.880 --> 01:34:27.190
were really just in my way and even teammates

2430
01:34:27.190 --> 01:34:28.023
were obstacles to him.

2431
01:34:28.023 --> 01:34:29.440
Right, so no wonder he ends up

2432
01:34:29.440 --> 01:34:32.010
as a single skull rower right.

2433
01:34:32.010 --> 01:34:34.650
But that doesn't work when you got four guys

2434
01:34:34.650 --> 01:34:37.600
who have to live with each other for a month plus

2435
01:34:37.600 --> 01:34:38.830
in the open sea.

2436
01:34:38.830 --> 01:34:41.930
But I think I, to this day I think,

2437
01:34:41.930 --> 01:34:43.710
maybe him getting sick was the best thing

2438
01:34:43.710 --> 01:34:45.190
that could have happened for him and the team

2439
01:34:45.190 --> 01:34:50.140
because he saw yep, exactly. And he saw what the team did.

2440
01:34:50.140 --> 01:34:53.430
We picked them up, seamlessly myself, Angus, Alex.

2441
01:34:53.430 --> 01:34:55.000
It wasn't a, just so you know,

2442
01:34:55.000 --> 01:34:56.180
we're rowing your hours right now. None of that.

2443
01:34:56.180 --> 01:34:58.210
And I mean, he fell asleep. He was delirious.

2444
01:34:58.210 --> 01:34:59.810
I mean he woke up and he didn't realize

2445
01:34:59.810 --> 01:35:00.715
how long he'd been out.

2446
01:35:00.715 --> 01:35:04.910
We got it, and by the way, stay in there. We got you still.

2447
01:35:04.910 --> 01:35:08.450
And I think this was, this was a game changer for him.

2448
01:35:08.450 --> 01:35:11.260
I mean, when he got better and he did and he got stronger,

2449
01:35:11.260 --> 01:35:13.940
but it took them a week plus,

2450
01:35:13.940 --> 01:35:16.427
he felt he had to live up to a makeup for it.

2451
01:35:16.427 --> 01:35:17.455
And he didn't of course,

2452
01:35:17.455 --> 01:35:18.570
because that's not what teams are about,

2453
01:35:18.570 --> 01:35:23.060
but he was so humbled and so appreciative

2454
01:35:23.060 --> 01:35:26.180
that he became, he became an animal out there.

2455
01:35:26.180 --> 01:35:27.013
I mean, it was insane.

2456
01:35:27.013 --> 01:35:28.297
And when we were chasing the world record

2457
01:35:28.297 --> 01:35:29.801
and it was coming down to the hour,

2458
01:35:29.801 --> 01:35:31.340
he was that differentiate.

2459
01:35:31.340 --> 01:35:33.030
I mean, he was rowing for six hours straight

2460
01:35:33.030 --> 01:35:34.190
without stopping.

2461
01:35:34.190 --> 01:35:35.030
It was insane.

2462
01:35:35.030 --> 01:35:38.963
Yeah. So you guys didn't have the most auspicious start,

2463
01:35:39.830 --> 01:35:41.300
but you kind of find your groove,

2464
01:35:41.300 --> 01:35:42.690
you get some good weather.

2465
01:35:42.690 --> 01:35:45.010
You're throwing it down pretty hard.

2466
01:35:45.010 --> 01:35:46.010
You got some storm, I mean,

2467
01:35:46.010 --> 01:35:47.080
there's all kinds of shit that happened.

2468
01:35:47.080 --> 01:35:48.750
Day 50, there's some crazy storm,

2469
01:35:48.750 --> 01:35:51.500
or day, what day was that? Day?

2470
01:35:51.500 --> 01:35:53.500
30, it was probably around day 30.

2471
01:35:53.500 --> 01:35:54.670
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

2472
01:35:54.670 --> 01:35:57.740
But then you just throw the hammer down in these final days

2473
01:35:57.740 --> 01:36:02.690
and you're just busting out like 80, 90 mile days to,

2474
01:36:02.690 --> 01:36:06.800
come in and eclipse the world record by pretty good margin.

2475
01:36:06.800 --> 01:36:07.943
Yeah. I mean,

2476
01:36:10.800 --> 01:36:13.720
without giving too much away with the doc and stuff,

2477
01:36:13.720 --> 01:36:17.260
but I think the pinnacle of my athletic career

2478
01:36:17.260 --> 01:36:20.620
came at that moment on day 30, we get knocked by this storm

2479
01:36:20.620 --> 01:36:22.920
that should be there, pushes us backwards.

2480
01:36:22.920 --> 01:36:25.950
We have to anchor for just a few hours.

2481
01:36:25.950 --> 01:36:27.970
But when, when you're talking about,

2482
01:36:27.970 --> 01:36:31.070
barely being ahead of the world's record, this matters,

2483
01:36:31.070 --> 01:36:33.110
and finally the storms kind of starting to pass.

2484
01:36:33.110 --> 01:36:36.400
We're able to pull up the pair anchor, start rowing again.

2485
01:36:36.400 --> 01:36:40.593
And at one point we were going 0.8 knots, 0.8 knots.

2486
01:36:40.593 --> 01:36:44.100
That is you can crawl on your hands and knees at about that.

2487
01:36:44.100 --> 01:36:46.580
How many days did that go on for?

2488
01:36:46.580 --> 01:36:47.413
It was only--

2489
01:36:47.413 --> 01:36:48.640
Ended you just ended up dropping anchor.

2490
01:36:48.640 --> 01:36:50.900
Yeah, basically it started the day before

2491
01:36:50.900 --> 01:36:55.900
on January 12th and it slowed us down,

2492
01:36:56.130 --> 01:36:57.630
slowed us down until finally January 13th,

2493
01:36:57.630 --> 01:37:01.430
which was Friday the 13th. I'm not superstitious until now.

2494
01:37:01.430 --> 01:37:03.360
Now I am, but it hit us hard.

2495
01:37:03.360 --> 01:37:04.400
And that's when it just slows down.

2496
01:37:04.400 --> 01:37:06.930
We had to, we couldn't make any forward progress.

2497
01:37:06.930 --> 01:37:07.763
So we had to drop the anchor.

2498
01:37:07.763 --> 01:37:09.667
So we wouldn't get pushed back.

2499
01:37:09.667 --> 01:37:11.760
We're only in anchor for a handful of hours,

2500
01:37:11.760 --> 01:37:14.640
but it's enough because not only are we not going anywhere,

2501
01:37:14.640 --> 01:37:16.720
but like we've been not going anywhere

2502
01:37:16.720 --> 01:37:17.775
for about a day and a half now.

2503
01:37:17.775 --> 01:37:21.640
So we start rowing I'm in the cabin

2504
01:37:21.640 --> 01:37:24.680
and I'm having to do the math, what has this storm like,

2505
01:37:24.680 --> 01:37:26.210
what are the repercussions of this storm?

2506
01:37:26.210 --> 01:37:27.580
What's it done to us?

2507
01:37:27.580 --> 01:37:29.480
It ends up being really, really simple math,

2508
01:37:29.480 --> 01:37:31.440
I'm checking it over and over because I got

2509
01:37:31.440 --> 01:37:33.540
you get this kind of mental fog when you're out there.

2510
01:37:33.540 --> 01:37:35.580
Cause you just have such little stimulation

2511
01:37:35.580 --> 01:37:38.370
plus you're sleep deprived and malnourished and dehydrated.

2512
01:37:38.370 --> 01:37:41.440
But it's really simple math. We've got basically,

2513
01:37:41.440 --> 01:37:45.380
almost exactly to the mile 400 miles left to go

2514
01:37:45.380 --> 01:37:48.010
and almost exactly to the hour, five days to do it.

2515
01:37:48.010 --> 01:37:50.800
So it becomes 80 mile days.

2516
01:37:50.800 --> 01:37:53.050
We have to basically achieve 80 miles a day

2517
01:37:53.050 --> 01:37:54.050
for the next five days.

2518
01:37:54.050 --> 01:37:56.870
And then like, it'll be close if we do that.

2519
01:37:56.870 --> 01:37:58.510
And just to put in perspective,

2520
01:37:58.510 --> 01:38:01.520
70 mile days is world record pace at that point,

2521
01:38:01.520 --> 01:38:03.061
like that was what would have done it.

2522
01:38:03.061 --> 01:38:05.943
So like I'm in the cabin, I'm just,

2523
01:38:06.847 --> 01:38:09.800
I'm upset and I'm starting to cry a little bit.

2524
01:38:09.800 --> 01:38:11.640
I'm just, this isn't fair. We've worked too hard.

2525
01:38:11.640 --> 01:38:14.166
These guys have worked too hard and,

2526
01:38:14.166 --> 01:38:16.750
kind of like compose myself a little

2527
01:38:16.750 --> 01:38:18.571
'cause I know they're waiting for me

2528
01:38:18.571 --> 01:38:19.529
to come out there with the,

2529
01:38:19.529 --> 01:38:21.212
with the number and I come out and

2530
01:38:21.212 --> 01:38:23.450
I've got these three expectant faces looking at me.

2531
01:38:23.450 --> 01:38:27.170
And I tell them what I tell them

2532
01:38:27.170 --> 01:38:29.421
and the responses were just so interesting.

2533
01:38:29.421 --> 01:38:30.495
I'll never forget.

2534
01:38:30.495 --> 01:38:31.980
First of all, Matt, who,

2535
01:38:31.980 --> 01:38:35.880
seasick and kind of comes back, roaring back, so to speak,

2536
01:38:35.880 --> 01:38:36.950
he goes, oh my God, thank God.

2537
01:38:36.950 --> 01:38:37.870
I thought you were gonna say a hundred.

2538
01:38:37.870 --> 01:38:41.150
Like this guy was genuinely relieved

2539
01:38:41.150 --> 01:38:42.830
that we only had to do 80 miles a day,

2540
01:38:42.830 --> 01:38:44.660
which is 10 miles above world record pace.

2541
01:38:44.660 --> 01:38:47.757
Like he believed so much that this team was capable of it,

2542
01:38:47.757 --> 01:38:50.297
that he was relieved that it was only 80.

2543
01:38:50.297 --> 01:38:53.410
And then Alex, the young kid process oriented guy,

2544
01:38:53.410 --> 01:38:55.853
he was just like, I'll do whatever it takes Skip.

2545
01:38:55.853 --> 01:38:57.880
He called me Skip all the time.

2546
01:38:57.880 --> 01:38:58.713
And said I'll like,

2547
01:38:58.713 --> 01:39:00.660
I'll row every hour of the night if I have to.

2548
01:39:00.660 --> 01:39:02.030
Meaning, and those were his worst nights,

2549
01:39:02.030 --> 01:39:03.860
he would literally pass out in the middle

2550
01:39:03.860 --> 01:39:05.550
of his rowing shift cause he'd be so tired,

2551
01:39:05.550 --> 01:39:06.650
just like blackout sometimes.

2552
01:39:06.650 --> 01:39:08.060
And all of a sudden I feel heavy and I look back

2553
01:39:08.060 --> 01:39:09.740
and he's like half the elbow.

2554
01:39:09.740 --> 01:39:11.390
I'm like, you've got to get up.

2555
01:39:11.390 --> 01:39:13.840
He was just letting us know that he would row

2556
01:39:13.840 --> 01:39:15.333
his worst hours if that's what it was taken.

2557
01:39:15.333 --> 01:39:16.310
Like he was all in.

2558
01:39:16.310 --> 01:39:21.310
And then Angus, he was just saying that this is what we did.

2559
01:39:22.210 --> 01:39:23.560
This is why we combined forces.

2560
01:39:23.560 --> 01:39:25.180
We want a chance at history.

2561
01:39:25.180 --> 01:39:27.860
It's not like a great one, but we've got one in it's hours.

2562
01:39:27.860 --> 01:39:31.580
And I thought, damn if that isn't the best response

2563
01:39:31.580 --> 01:39:33.220
of any three teammates you could ever have.

2564
01:39:33.220 --> 01:39:35.950
I mean, that was when I knew that,

2565
01:39:35.950 --> 01:39:38.360
like I had done what we needed to do.

2566
01:39:38.360 --> 01:39:40.540
I came back because I failed as a leader

2567
01:39:41.810 --> 01:39:43.247
with the first team.

2568
01:39:43.247 --> 01:39:45.630
And its more specifically with Greg,

2569
01:39:45.630 --> 01:39:47.090
the guy who left, who was healthy,

2570
01:39:47.090 --> 01:39:50.550
it's, I always say it's easy to get down on Greg

2571
01:39:50.550 --> 01:39:52.260
he left, he abandoned us and believe me,

2572
01:39:52.260 --> 01:39:53.950
you don't wanna say his name in front of my dad

2573
01:39:53.950 --> 01:39:56.020
because my dad can't stand him.

2574
01:39:56.020 --> 01:39:59.870
But in the end it was my fault.

2575
01:39:59.870 --> 01:40:01.460
I failed to get him to a place

2576
01:40:01.460 --> 01:40:02.963
that he wanted to be like Tom.

2577
01:40:03.954 --> 01:40:05.780
And I never could shake that.

2578
01:40:05.780 --> 01:40:07.990
And so that's why I came back and all of a sudden I've got

2579
01:40:07.990 --> 01:40:11.090
three guys that are willing to do whatever it takes.

2580
01:40:11.090 --> 01:40:14.160
And the next five days when they're, were already beat up,

2581
01:40:14.160 --> 01:40:15.920
I mean, we're so beat up.

2582
01:40:15.920 --> 01:40:17.620
And so I said, all right.

2583
01:40:17.620 --> 01:40:20.610
So we row the first of that five days

2584
01:40:20.610 --> 01:40:22.160
because we're not quite out of the storm.

2585
01:40:22.160 --> 01:40:25.560
We row three people at a time, instead of two it's three.

2586
01:40:25.560 --> 01:40:28.360
Which means instead of two hours on two hours off,

2587
01:40:28.360 --> 01:40:31.320
you've got two hours on 40 minutes of rest.

2588
01:40:31.320 --> 01:40:32.940
And I'm thinking to myself,

2589
01:40:32.940 --> 01:40:34.717
this is going to be a rough first day.

2590
01:40:34.717 --> 01:40:38.100
And we do it for 24 straight hours. And we row 79 miles,

2591
01:40:38.100 --> 01:40:40.350
which is good.

2592
01:40:40.350 --> 01:40:42.220
Good, but you're behind.

2593
01:40:42.220 --> 01:40:43.623
But yeah, for putting all that much effort into it

2594
01:40:43.623 --> 01:40:46.260
were still one mile off of our pace.

2595
01:40:46.260 --> 01:40:48.420
And I just think that I'm thinking to myself,

2596
01:40:48.420 --> 01:40:50.700
I remember just as kind of like we're starting the next

2597
01:40:50.700 --> 01:40:53.130
official day, which I think we marked at 6:00 AM.

2598
01:40:53.130 --> 01:40:55.890
Was that the next official day for us?

2599
01:40:55.890 --> 01:40:58.990
I just remember thinking, like, I don't know.

2600
01:40:58.990 --> 01:41:01.500
I could see people taking their foot off the gas

2601
01:41:01.500 --> 01:41:02.333
a little bit right now.

2602
01:41:02.333 --> 01:41:03.980
Cause we're gonna go back to two on two off,

2603
01:41:03.980 --> 01:41:05.490
we're gonna go back to our normal shift pattern.

2604
01:41:05.490 --> 01:41:06.403
So I'm thinking to myself,

2605
01:41:06.403 --> 01:41:09.130
If you couldn't get 80 with three dudes.

2606
01:41:09.130 --> 01:41:10.320
How are you gonna do it with two.

2607
01:41:10.320 --> 01:41:11.810
Maybe you'll just--
On less rest.

2608
01:41:11.810 --> 01:41:14.500
Yeah a little bit off their foot off the gas.

2609
01:41:14.500 --> 01:41:16.030
I mean, I'm thinking to myself,

2610
01:41:16.030 --> 01:41:17.990
I got to make sure I don't do that.

2611
01:41:17.990 --> 01:41:20.420
Because now you're, now feeling a little defeated,

2612
01:41:20.420 --> 01:41:22.741
like I don't think we've got it.

2613
01:41:22.741 --> 01:41:25.410
But I can tell you what I know for a fact that nobody did,

2614
01:41:25.410 --> 01:41:30.200
because on that second day we did 94 miles.

2615
01:41:30.200 --> 01:41:34.130
Which just that's all that you needed to know

2616
01:41:34.130 --> 01:41:37.570
is that we did 95 miles and the third was 92.

2617
01:41:37.570 --> 01:41:40.140
So like five knots. What is that like?

2618
01:41:40.140 --> 01:41:41.290
Yeah, it was, no, it wasn't,

2619
01:41:41.290 --> 01:41:43.167
it wasn't quite five, but it was like, yeah,

2620
01:41:43.167 --> 01:41:48.110
it was so four knots will get you a 100 roughly.

2621
01:41:48.110 --> 01:41:49.600
Okay.
And so we were,

2622
01:41:49.600 --> 01:41:52.370
we're like in the high threes all day long

2623
01:41:52.370 --> 01:41:53.740
making quick shift changes,

2624
01:41:53.740 --> 01:41:55.280
like everything was in the thing is

2625
01:41:55.280 --> 01:41:56.812
nobody was talking about the world record pace.

2626
01:41:56.812 --> 01:41:58.140
That's the thing is like,

2627
01:41:58.140 --> 01:42:00.215
no one was saying like, alright,

2628
01:42:00.215 --> 01:42:01.960
where are we on the world record pace.

2629
01:42:01.960 --> 01:42:04.450
Like, it just became about like each other,

2630
01:42:04.450 --> 01:42:07.770
it just became about cooking extra food for everybody.

2631
01:42:07.770 --> 01:42:08.890
Hey, what does everybody want?

2632
01:42:08.890 --> 01:42:10.300
I'm gonna cook five meals right now.

2633
01:42:10.300 --> 01:42:11.230
So that you've got some,

2634
01:42:11.230 --> 01:42:13.330
I'm going to put it right next to your seat. Boom, eat it.

2635
01:42:13.330 --> 01:42:16.633
I had this infected heel, thing hurts so bad.

2636
01:42:16.633 --> 01:42:19.250
It was, it was so infected like even just barely touching it

2637
01:42:19.250 --> 01:42:22.790
like really, really like kind of sent like a pain up my leg.

2638
01:42:22.790 --> 01:42:25.390
And I'm notorious for not taking care

2639
01:42:25.390 --> 01:42:28.180
of my body very well out there when I should.

2640
01:42:28.180 --> 01:42:29.707
And Angus always yells at me for that.

2641
01:42:29.707 --> 01:42:32.429
And he would do little self things like hey Jay,

2642
01:42:32.429 --> 01:42:34.380
when you get back in the cabin, I laid it out.

2643
01:42:34.380 --> 01:42:37.230
You don't have to do anything. The gauze is there.

2644
01:42:37.230 --> 01:42:38.210
The wrap is there.

2645
01:42:38.210 --> 01:42:41.420
All you need to do is just simply take off the thing,

2646
01:42:41.420 --> 01:42:42.920
clean it wrap it done.

2647
01:42:42.920 --> 01:42:45.650
And like, he would just, he was encouraging me to do

2648
01:42:45.650 --> 01:42:47.490
what I needed to do to take care of my body.

2649
01:42:47.490 --> 01:42:48.800
And that was what everybody was doing.

2650
01:42:48.800 --> 01:42:50.230
Nobody was talking about themselves.

2651
01:42:50.230 --> 01:42:51.960
Oh, I'm so tired guys. I'm so beat up.

2652
01:42:51.960 --> 01:42:53.850
It was all about how are you doing?

2653
01:42:53.850 --> 01:42:55.640
And that was to this day.

2654
01:42:55.640 --> 01:42:57.030
It's the pinnacle of my athletic career.

2655
01:42:57.030 --> 01:43:00.200
That, those five days. And I was, I honestly,

2656
01:43:00.200 --> 01:43:03.240
no one's gonna believe this, but I didn't care anymore

2657
01:43:03.240 --> 01:43:06.060
if we broke the world record, I didn't.

2658
01:43:06.060 --> 01:43:07.179
We did we ended up doing it.

2659
01:43:07.179 --> 01:43:10.710
But in that moment, I'm thinking to myself, this is it.

2660
01:43:10.710 --> 01:43:13.720
This is what, this is what I was trying so hard for it.

2661
01:43:13.720 --> 01:43:15.953
I chased this like idea of

2662
01:43:15.953 --> 01:43:17.960
the only time I'm gonna be accepted,

2663
01:43:17.960 --> 01:43:19.740
I'm gonna accept myself or I'm gonna be accepted

2664
01:43:19.740 --> 01:43:22.610
as an elite athlete, a winner is if, if it's a gold medal,

2665
01:43:22.610 --> 01:43:23.610
if it's a world record.

2666
01:43:23.610 --> 01:43:26.303
And you start to realize like no one gives a shit,

2667
01:43:26.303 --> 01:43:27.683
like it doesn't matter about that.

2668
01:43:27.683 --> 01:43:30.870
What it matters is about is that when you step off that boat

2669
01:43:30.870 --> 01:43:33.550
or when you cross that finish line, that you know that,

2670
01:43:33.550 --> 01:43:37.020
that you led the team in the right way and that you gave

2671
01:43:37.020 --> 01:43:39.210
everything that you had and they did as well,

2672
01:43:39.210 --> 01:43:41.750
because of all this stuff that we've been talking about.

2673
01:43:41.750 --> 01:43:44.270
Yeah, yeah. That's beautifully put.

2674
01:43:44.270 --> 01:43:47.760
So then with that experience in breaking the world record

2675
01:43:47.760 --> 01:43:52.480
and having this, extraordinary kind of camaraderie

2676
01:43:52.480 --> 01:43:54.930
with your teammates, like, what do you make of that.

2677
01:43:54.930 --> 01:43:57.433
Like how do you extrapolate upon that to,

2678
01:44:00.090 --> 01:44:03.700
communicate to the average person or somebody

2679
01:44:03.700 --> 01:44:06.040
who's striving to achieve a goal or somebody

2680
01:44:06.040 --> 01:44:10.013
who is a leader in their respective employment.

2681
01:44:12.180 --> 01:44:13.720
I have a lot of thoughts around that,

2682
01:44:13.720 --> 01:44:15.630
but I'll take it to its most basic level.

2683
01:44:15.630 --> 01:44:19.410 line:15%
And I'll say it by this, like we hear this cliche

2684
01:44:19.410 --> 01:44:21.097 line:15%
all the time that life is short, right?

2685
01:44:21.097 --> 01:44:23.760 line:15%
We always life is short. Life is short.

2686
01:44:23.760 --> 01:44:26.840 line:15%
I don't actually believe that life is short or at least

2687
01:44:26.840 --> 01:44:28.410
I don't think we actually believe it.

2688
01:44:28.410 --> 01:44:29.810
I mean, whether life is long or short,

2689
01:44:29.810 --> 01:44:32.319
I guess just depends on what you compare it to.

2690
01:44:32.319 --> 01:44:34.010
If you compare it to a giant Redwood, it's short,

2691
01:44:34.010 --> 01:44:35.980
but if you compare it to like a fly its long,

2692
01:44:35.980 --> 01:44:38.580
whatever it is, but what do we believe?

2693
01:44:38.580 --> 01:44:41.430
And I think most people to include myself at times,

2694
01:44:41.430 --> 01:44:42.660
we believe life is long.

2695
01:44:42.660 --> 01:44:44.150
It's actually so long in fact

2696
01:44:44.150 --> 01:44:47.356
that we put off these things, right.

2697
01:44:47.356 --> 01:44:49.080
We always wanted to take piano lessons,

2698
01:44:49.080 --> 01:44:51.240
but we'll do it next year when my life isn't as busy.

2699
01:44:51.240 --> 01:44:53.190
We do all these different things.

2700
01:44:53.190 --> 01:44:55.970
I'm gonna patch things up with my,

2701
01:44:55.970 --> 01:44:57.960
my mom or my dad. I haven't talked to in a while,

2702
01:44:57.960 --> 01:44:59.730
I'll do it next week. I'll make the call next week,

2703
01:44:59.730 --> 01:45:00.590
next month, whatever it is.

2704
01:45:00.590 --> 01:45:02.550
And we put things off because we honestly believe

2705
01:45:02.550 --> 01:45:03.877
we have more time.

2706
01:45:03.877 --> 01:45:06.730
And the reality is is that,

2707
01:45:06.730 --> 01:45:09.730
that is exactly what keeps us from doing these things,

2708
01:45:09.730 --> 01:45:12.430
is it, we always think we've got more time.

2709
01:45:12.430 --> 01:45:15.883
And so if I'm talking about, at the most basic level,

2710
01:45:17.190 --> 01:45:19.650
when we start to attempt these things,

2711
01:45:19.650 --> 01:45:22.420
we should honestly have a deep seated belief

2712
01:45:22.420 --> 01:45:25.120
that life is finite, it's short and it's fleeting.

2713
01:45:25.120 --> 01:45:28.590
And in which case we will start to act now.

2714
01:45:28.590 --> 01:45:29.659
I think if we're talking about,

2715
01:45:29.659 --> 01:45:30.950
when we're starting to talk about,

2716
01:45:30.950 --> 01:45:33.650
talk to the individual out there that's listening right now,

2717
01:45:33.650 --> 01:45:37.299
I'd say that we need to start by how we think about time

2718
01:45:37.299 --> 01:45:40.310
and how we think about how we spend our time.

2719
01:45:40.310 --> 01:45:42.950
Because as we all know, weeks become months become years.

2720
01:45:42.950 --> 01:45:46.040
And all of a sudden that's how we spend our lives.

2721
01:45:46.040 --> 01:45:49.423
And I think I'm, I'm afraid,

2722
01:45:50.310 --> 01:45:53.450
I'm fearful that I'm not gonna be able to get

2723
01:45:53.450 --> 01:45:55.880
these things done because I'm running out of time.

2724
01:45:55.880 --> 01:45:57.697
And that helps me.

2725
01:45:57.697 --> 01:46:02.360
And I think, I hope that helps other people as well.

2726
01:46:02.360 --> 01:46:03.563
Yeah, that's amazing.

2727
01:46:05.330 --> 01:46:07.890
Dude we haven't even talked about rowing the Pacific yet.

2728
01:46:07.890 --> 01:46:08.723
What time is it?

2729
01:46:10.490 --> 01:46:12.860
You're just a month out basically of doing

2730
01:46:12.860 --> 01:46:15.453
the craziest thing you've done to date.

2731
01:46:16.372 --> 01:46:18.820
So, so walk me through the process of,

2732
01:46:18.820 --> 01:46:22.840
of getting enthusiastic about this challenge,

2733
01:46:22.840 --> 01:46:26.500
this idea that perhaps even after setting this world record,

2734
01:46:26.500 --> 01:46:27.333
that you weren't done,

2735
01:46:27.333 --> 01:46:29.140
that there was this other challenge out there,

2736
01:46:29.140 --> 01:46:33.040
that you wanted to tackle and putting the team together.

2737
01:46:33.040 --> 01:46:34.440
Like the audience is gonna kill me

2738
01:46:34.440 --> 01:46:37.700
if I don't ask you like how you train for all this stuff.

2739
01:46:37.700 --> 01:46:41.090
So yeah walk me up to this, to this,

2740
01:46:41.090 --> 01:46:45.660
latest adventure and, everything that went into it.

2741
01:46:45.660 --> 01:46:47.620
Yeah. I mean, I feel like I've gotten all these

2742
01:46:47.620 --> 01:46:49.720
new tools and lessons you keep using the metaphor

2743
01:46:49.720 --> 01:46:51.857
that I'm basically getting a master's in leadership here.

2744
01:46:51.857 --> 01:46:53.030
And so that's why I feel like,

2745
01:46:53.030 --> 01:46:55.310
I feel like I just graduated after this Atlantic row

2746
01:46:55.310 --> 01:46:58.170
with a master's and now I wanna go apply it.

2747
01:46:58.170 --> 01:46:59.700
And that's really where the motivation

2748
01:46:59.700 --> 01:47:00.560
from the Pacific came.

2749
01:47:00.560 --> 01:47:05.560
Is I've seen this idea of leveraging each other's

2750
01:47:05.840 --> 01:47:07.580
human emotions to build this community,

2751
01:47:07.580 --> 01:47:08.630
to build all this trust,

2752
01:47:08.630 --> 01:47:10.000
all things we've been talking about it,

2753
01:47:10.000 --> 01:47:11.040
I've seen how it works.

2754
01:47:11.040 --> 01:47:13.390
And now I wanna just go ahead and leverage

2755
01:47:13.390 --> 01:47:14.870
the hell out of it,

2756
01:47:14.870 --> 01:47:16.900
because I think that this is going to be...

2757
01:47:16.900 --> 01:47:19.450
Like you find, the lights have gone on you finally got it.

2758
01:47:19.450 --> 01:47:22.380
And now you could actually return and execute

2759
01:47:22.380 --> 01:47:24.794
at the highest level, because you've got the skills,

2760
01:47:24.794 --> 01:47:28.670
the experience, the endurance you've met the obstacles.

2761
01:47:28.670 --> 01:47:29.503
all of it.

2762
01:47:29.503 --> 01:47:31.120
Yeah. And that's what, and that's where we're at.

2763
01:47:31.120 --> 01:47:33.670
So, I live in the San Francisco bay area.

2764
01:47:33.670 --> 01:47:35.140
The Pacific is my backyard.

2765
01:47:35.140 --> 01:47:37.040
And so what better thing to have,

2766
01:47:37.040 --> 01:47:39.112
to enter the race that leaves from your backyard

2767
01:47:39.112 --> 01:47:40.270
and goes to Hawaii.

2768
01:47:40.270 --> 01:47:44.820
So that's where the motivation comes from.

2769
01:47:44.820 --> 01:47:47.923
I'm excited to build a new team.

2770
01:47:48.830 --> 01:47:51.660
Why a new team though. Why not just get all these guys?

2771
01:47:51.660 --> 01:47:53.730
Yeah.
You got your guy.

2772
01:47:53.730 --> 01:47:56.353
But those guys are, their whys are changing.

2773
01:47:56.353 --> 01:48:00.050
People are living their lives. How dare they?

2774
01:48:00.050 --> 01:48:02.900
I thought that was selfish. I'm glad you think the same.

2775
01:48:02.900 --> 01:48:04.330
Well Matt, I've never seen anyone

2776
01:48:04.330 --> 01:48:06.830
have more closure than Matt.

2777
01:48:06.830 --> 01:48:11.830
Matt after this row and the doc does a great job of kind of,

2778
01:48:12.230 --> 01:48:15.130
of showing this, but Matt has so much closure

2779
01:48:15.130 --> 01:48:19.730
after this row, both athletically his athletic career,

2780
01:48:19.730 --> 01:48:21.060
but also his personal life.

2781
01:48:21.060 --> 01:48:24.320
This idea of being able to really kind of close the loop

2782
01:48:24.320 --> 01:48:25.977
on his, on a lot of the things.

2783
01:48:25.977 --> 01:48:29.230
He has no interest and believe me, I tried.

2784
01:48:29.230 --> 01:48:31.390
But he's done. He's like, Jay, I'm done.

2785
01:48:31.390 --> 01:48:32.330
I am done.

2786
01:48:32.330 --> 01:48:33.820
He learned what he needed to learn.

2787
01:48:33.820 --> 01:48:35.290
He's out.
He got that closure.

2788
01:48:35.290 --> 01:48:38.880
He had that experience and now he can cut the ties

2789
01:48:38.880 --> 01:48:41.730
and build a new life on top of that.

2790
01:48:41.730 --> 01:48:42.563
Yeah, exactly.

2791
01:48:42.563 --> 01:48:44.237
And that's exactly what he did.

2792
01:48:44.237 --> 01:48:48.164
They had another kid and they're living a happy life

2793
01:48:48.164 --> 01:48:49.470
in Texas right now.

2794
01:48:49.470 --> 01:48:51.260
So--
But Angus?

2795
01:48:51.260 --> 01:48:52.830
Well Angus, isn't going anywhere.

2796
01:48:52.830 --> 01:48:55.113
Holler at my boy. He's my dude.

2797
01:48:56.061 --> 01:48:58.280
Yeah. He's the best.

2798
01:48:58.280 --> 01:49:01.115
He's, this is his life and for him,

2799
01:49:01.115 --> 01:49:03.180
his motivation is different again,

2800
01:49:03.180 --> 01:49:05.223
this is for another time but,

2801
01:49:06.225 --> 01:49:08.830
he's more comfortable, he's a guy has always been

2802
01:49:08.830 --> 01:49:12.153
more comfortable out at sea than he was indoors.

2803
01:49:12.153 --> 01:49:14.627
I mean, I like to come home. I love these things.

2804
01:49:14.627 --> 01:49:16.220
But I also like to come home,

2805
01:49:16.220 --> 01:49:20.190
for me, it's I wanna see my wife, I wanna see my son,

2806
01:49:20.190 --> 01:49:23.940
I wanna spend time with friends and family

2807
01:49:23.940 --> 01:49:26.010
and go golfing and run my business

2808
01:49:26.010 --> 01:49:27.160
I want to do these things.

2809
01:49:27.160 --> 01:49:30.670
But Angus always seems like tough to reenter,

2810
01:49:30.670 --> 01:49:33.780
like he always has a hard time coming back and I think

2811
01:49:35.544 --> 01:49:37.960
he's, chasing something that would be his

2812
01:49:37.960 --> 01:49:39.080
that's for him to answer not me,

2813
01:49:39.080 --> 01:49:42.210
but he was ready. So he was in, so it was me and him.

2814
01:49:42.210 --> 01:49:44.803
And now we're looking for two other guys and,

2815
01:49:46.510 --> 01:49:48.340
one of his first teammates from the first time

2816
01:49:48.340 --> 01:49:51.650
in the Atlantic Gus Barton was in.

2817
01:49:51.650 --> 01:49:54.550
And he's a he's a personal trainer in London

2818
01:49:54.550 --> 01:49:56.680
and a great athlete and has experience.

2819
01:49:56.680 --> 01:49:59.600
And then Duncan Roy was our next guy

2820
01:49:59.600 --> 01:50:01.970
who had rowed the Atlantic twice already as well.

2821
01:50:01.970 --> 01:50:03.970
And his big motivation was that he didn't really feel like

2822
01:50:03.970 --> 01:50:07.590
he had the experience that he wanted to have

2823
01:50:07.590 --> 01:50:09.360
in both those rows for different reasons.

2824
01:50:09.360 --> 01:50:12.210
He was looking for an ultimate team experience.

2825
01:50:12.210 --> 01:50:15.020
And so we had this team, but COVID,

2826
01:50:15.020 --> 01:50:16.170
we're all sick of talking about COVID,

2827
01:50:16.170 --> 01:50:17.940
but it precluded us from doing it 2020,

2828
01:50:17.940 --> 01:50:18.987
which was when we were supposed to do it.

2829
01:50:18.987 --> 01:50:23.470
And so that comes and goes, and we now sit here.

2830
01:50:23.470 --> 01:50:25.490
We built ourselves up Gus has to,

2831
01:50:25.490 --> 01:50:28.560
has to bow out because his wife was planning on

2832
01:50:28.560 --> 01:50:31.150
growing they're growing their family and having a child.

2833
01:50:31.150 --> 01:50:33.910
Which I totally understand. So he can't be in for 2021.

2834
01:50:33.910 --> 01:50:36.350
So we're looking for this fourth guy.

2835
01:50:36.350 --> 01:50:39.430
And Duncan, we all put together a list of people

2836
01:50:39.430 --> 01:50:41.180
that we'd be interested in.

2837
01:50:41.180 --> 01:50:43.470
Duncan had a couple of guys on his list.

2838
01:50:43.470 --> 01:50:46.510
And one of those guys we ended up taking, which was,

2839
01:50:46.510 --> 01:50:49.410
which was Jordan Shuttleworth and again,

2840
01:50:49.410 --> 01:50:51.439
no experience on the ocean. So we're talking about--

2841
01:50:51.439 --> 01:50:53.320
You always give one guy who has no ocean experience.

2842
01:50:53.320 --> 01:50:54.175
We just wanna make sure that we make it

2843
01:50:54.175 --> 01:50:55.500
just a little bit harder for ourselves.

2844
01:50:55.500 --> 01:50:59.890
So yeah, he's a former Royal Marine,

2845
01:50:59.890 --> 01:51:02.653
big, strong athletic individual,

2846
01:51:03.580 --> 01:51:05.050
but no ocean rowing experience.

2847
01:51:05.050 --> 01:51:06.260
And so, here we are,

2848
01:51:06.260 --> 01:51:09.300
here's our team coming into this year,

2849
01:51:09.300 --> 01:51:11.950
myself, Angus, who are, who's at this point,

2850
01:51:11.950 --> 01:51:13.570
my brother in adventure at this,

2851
01:51:13.570 --> 01:51:14.690
we've done so much together.

2852
01:51:14.690 --> 01:51:17.903
And then Duncan, who I'd never rode with in a boat before

2853
01:51:17.903 --> 01:51:20.010
in terms of a race, but have now spent

2854
01:51:20.010 --> 01:51:21.840
a lot of time with and just absolutely loved.

2855
01:51:21.840 --> 01:51:23.540
And then Jordan.

2856
01:51:23.540 --> 01:51:26.540
Right so I'm less familiar with Duncan and Jordan

2857
01:51:26.540 --> 01:51:28.190
because they're not in the documentary.

2858
01:51:28.190 --> 01:51:31.600
Because the documentary ends before the Pacific row,

2859
01:51:31.600 --> 01:51:34.460
but both military guys, which is interesting.

2860
01:51:34.460 --> 01:51:35.870
Yeah.

2861
01:51:35.870 --> 01:51:38.680
But also I trust come from some kind of

2862
01:51:38.680 --> 01:51:41.803
competitive rowing background or just like ocean dudes.

2863
01:51:43.450 --> 01:51:44.570
Duncan was ocean rowing.

2864
01:51:44.570 --> 01:51:45.947
He'd done two ocean crossings already

2865
01:51:45.947 --> 01:51:48.670
and really has fallen in love with it.

2866
01:51:48.670 --> 01:51:49.503
And Jordan, no,

2867
01:51:49.503 --> 01:51:52.490
no like really no nautical background.

2868
01:51:52.490 --> 01:51:55.590
Right, he's just a bad-ass Marine commando

2869
01:51:55.590 --> 01:51:56.926
who's tough as nails.

2870
01:51:56.926 --> 01:51:58.730
And never rowed in the ocean.

2871
01:51:58.730 --> 01:52:00.330
And you're like, that's our guy.

2872
01:52:01.800 --> 01:52:03.330
Yeah, exactly.

2873
01:52:03.330 --> 01:52:04.840
Yeah. Like I haven't learned my lesson.

2874
01:52:04.840 --> 01:52:06.849
Well, it's just, it was,

2875
01:52:06.849 --> 01:52:10.980
we interviewed quite a few people to include people

2876
01:52:10.980 --> 01:52:14.393
that had ocean rowing experience, but he just seemed,

2877
01:52:15.510 --> 01:52:17.060
he just, he had that.

2878
01:52:17.060 --> 01:52:18.890
I'll tell you what I loved about Jordan.

2879
01:52:18.890 --> 01:52:20.610
I mean, there's a lot of things I love about Jordan.

2880
01:52:20.610 --> 01:52:23.710
Of course, especially now that we finished one together,

2881
01:52:23.710 --> 01:52:26.150
we're like brothers, but the first thing he said,

2882
01:52:26.150 --> 01:52:28.140
when we got on our first interview with him,

2883
01:52:28.140 --> 01:52:31.660
Zoom of course, and he says, I'm nervous.

2884
01:52:31.660 --> 01:52:32.493
What are you, what are you nervous?

2885
01:52:32.493 --> 01:52:34.840
He's like, I just want this so bad.

2886
01:52:34.840 --> 01:52:38.580
I just thought that is a great, I liked that,

2887
01:52:38.580 --> 01:52:42.600
that he there's already so much on it for him.

2888
01:52:42.600 --> 01:52:43.511
And he, I asked him, I said,

2889
01:52:43.511 --> 01:52:44.930
do you remember the first thing you said?

2890
01:52:44.930 --> 01:52:46.430
And he couldn't remember.

2891
01:52:46.430 --> 01:52:47.770
And I just remember it came from the heart.

2892
01:52:47.770 --> 01:52:50.120
He was just, he looked nervous and I just loved that.

2893
01:52:50.120 --> 01:52:52.369
It meant something to him.

2894
01:52:52.369 --> 01:52:55.170
The interesting thing about Angus is,

2895
01:52:55.170 --> 01:52:57.270
I looked into his background a little bit,

2896
01:52:58.160 --> 01:53:00.580
in addition to being the youngest ever to row

2897
01:53:00.580 --> 01:53:03.620
the Indian Atlantic and Pacific,

2898
01:53:03.620 --> 01:53:07.167
the guy has dealt with depression and ADHD.

2899
01:53:07.167 --> 01:53:10.630
And some, he's got some dark chapters in his past

2900
01:53:10.630 --> 01:53:12.880
that he had to kind of face and overcome.

2901
01:53:12.880 --> 01:53:14.363
Yeah and this has been,

2902
01:53:15.920 --> 01:53:17.510
something that we've constantly talked about.

2903
01:53:17.510 --> 01:53:20.050
I mean, we rowed the Atlantic together,

2904
01:53:20.050 --> 01:53:20.900
broke the world record.

2905
01:53:20.900 --> 01:53:24.030
Then a year later, we trekked across Namibia together.

2906
01:53:24.030 --> 01:53:24.990
Something that we don't talk a lot about,

2907
01:53:24.990 --> 01:53:28.209
but that was an incredible vacation,

2908
01:53:28.209 --> 01:53:30.528
a vacation with adventure.

2909
01:53:30.528 --> 01:53:31.555
(indistinct conversation)

2910
01:53:31.555 --> 01:53:33.120
That was anything but.

2911
01:53:33.120 --> 01:53:34.870
He didn't actually complete that one because

2912
01:53:34.870 --> 01:53:38.060
he got heat stroke and passed out and hit his head.

2913
01:53:38.060 --> 01:53:41.150
And then after that, he went into a tough time.

2914
01:53:41.150 --> 01:53:42.870
And there was actually about a six month stint

2915
01:53:42.870 --> 01:53:43.810
where I couldn't get ahold of him.

2916
01:53:43.810 --> 01:53:45.560
Most of his friends couldn't get ahold of him

2917
01:53:45.560 --> 01:53:48.010
and I'm the U.S. and he's in the UK.

2918
01:53:48.010 --> 01:53:50.695
So I'd be calling him and Alex,

2919
01:53:50.695 --> 01:53:54.420
our team mate from the Atlantic actually was trying so hard

2920
01:53:54.420 --> 01:53:56.450
to get ahold of him and he wouldn't respond to any of us.

2921
01:53:56.450 --> 01:53:58.820
So Alex somehow had one of his old credit cards

2922
01:53:58.820 --> 01:54:00.110
started using it at the bars.

2923
01:54:00.110 --> 01:54:01.810
He's like, well, if he's not gonna answer my phone calls,

2924
01:54:01.810 --> 01:54:03.510
I'm just gonna start charging the hell out of this thing

2925
01:54:03.510 --> 01:54:05.600
until he calls me pissed off.

2926
01:54:05.600 --> 01:54:07.153
But we and as it turned out,

2927
01:54:07.153 --> 01:54:10.070
what we learned is that he was just, he had,

2928
01:54:10.070 --> 01:54:12.380
he was wrestling with a lot of tough thoughts

2929
01:54:12.380 --> 01:54:15.444
and he's struggled with depression.

2930
01:54:15.444 --> 01:54:17.229
He's now in a much better situation.

2931
01:54:17.229 --> 01:54:21.336
He's in a very healthy relationship with a wonderful woman.

2932
01:54:21.336 --> 01:54:25.560
And, but it's not gone, he still has to deal with it.

2933
01:54:25.560 --> 01:54:29.240
And so it's, again that's his story,

2934
01:54:29.240 --> 01:54:30.510
but it's something that,

2935
01:54:30.510 --> 01:54:32.563
we've, we have to constantly talk about because

2936
01:54:32.563 --> 01:54:35.005
that's going to affect the that's going to affect

2937
01:54:35.005 --> 01:54:36.290
us being out there.

2938
01:54:36.290 --> 01:54:41.290
Yeah. I saw a piece, I think it was Vice that you did,

2939
01:54:41.760 --> 01:54:44.030
or it was kind of like a mini doc and it was you guys like

2940
01:54:44.030 --> 01:54:48.210
getting ready for this race and it feels like in this case,

2941
01:54:48.210 --> 01:54:50.750
you guys were together more or were those

2942
01:54:50.750 --> 01:54:52.587
just training partners that live out near you?

2943
01:54:52.587 --> 01:54:54.580
These are my training partners.

2944
01:54:54.580 --> 01:54:55.940
I wish those are my teammates.

2945
01:54:55.940 --> 01:54:57.990
I mean glad that my buddies helped out, but I mean,

2946
01:54:57.990 --> 01:55:00.370
that's what happens is that, especially with COVID,

2947
01:55:00.370 --> 01:55:02.770
we got together, I met Jordan face to face

2948
01:55:02.770 --> 01:55:04.920
for the first time when he got to San Francisco.

2949
01:55:04.920 --> 01:55:05.753
Wow.

2950
01:55:05.753 --> 01:55:10.090
Those I've got San Francisco Bear. I got, I've got,

2951
01:55:10.090 --> 01:55:11.810
training buddies, Mike Voksichu,

2952
01:55:11.810 --> 01:55:14.827
I rowed with in college. He goes out in the water with me,

2953
01:55:14.827 --> 01:55:18.860
And I got Kevin Grant, who's a collegiate football player.

2954
01:55:18.860 --> 01:55:21.610
So I do the weights with him and I got swimming partners,

2955
01:55:21.610 --> 01:55:23.000
trail running partners.

2956
01:55:23.000 --> 01:55:25.100
So those guys helped me get in shape.

2957
01:55:25.100 --> 01:55:27.180
I've got my committee that's great,

2958
01:55:27.180 --> 01:55:31.840
but we didn't, have any time to train together.

2959
01:55:31.840 --> 01:55:33.630
So we, you get to San Francisco,

2960
01:55:33.630 --> 01:55:35.610
we got two weeks until this row starts here.

2961
01:55:35.610 --> 01:55:38.493
This is this past may. Boat's not ready.

2962
01:55:40.810 --> 01:55:43.031
And not nearly as ready as we'd like it to be.

2963
01:55:43.031 --> 01:55:46.250
We haven't even all really spent any time together.

2964
01:55:46.250 --> 01:55:48.730
COVID slowing everything down because even at this point,

2965
01:55:48.730 --> 01:55:51.150
we're still struggling with stuff and we're way,

2966
01:55:51.150 --> 01:55:54.550
way over budget and underfunded.

2967
01:55:54.550 --> 01:55:55.950
So I mean, this is the team that shows you--

2968
01:55:55.950 --> 01:55:58.620
So you as a guy who's taught at business schools

2969
01:55:58.620 --> 01:56:01.650
and has all this wisdom about teammate team building

2970
01:56:01.650 --> 01:56:04.330
and leadership, you're breaking all your rules.

2971
01:56:04.330 --> 01:56:05.637
It's, it's. Yeah.

2972
01:56:05.637 --> 01:56:07.628
And, again, we got to say,

2973
01:56:07.628 --> 01:56:10.790
I could sit here and give you a litany of excuses.

2974
01:56:10.790 --> 01:56:12.830
Hey, well, it was COVID this and COVID that.

2975
01:56:12.830 --> 01:56:14.310
And in the end, it comes down to me.

2976
01:56:14.310 --> 01:56:16.100
In the end, it is my fault.

2977
01:56:16.100 --> 01:56:17.860
So we're not ready. We're not prepared.

2978
01:56:17.860 --> 01:56:19.260
We didn't raise enough money for this thing.

2979
01:56:19.260 --> 01:56:20.380
We didn't get enough sponsorship.

2980
01:56:20.380 --> 01:56:21.570
Well, whatever, this is my,

2981
01:56:21.570 --> 01:56:25.600
I have this team and this boat and this race ahead of us.

2982
01:56:25.600 --> 01:56:28.110
And those two weeks were not ideal to be fair.

2983
01:56:28.110 --> 01:56:31.578
And as we led up to this row, and so May were, instead of,

2984
01:56:31.578 --> 01:56:33.920
I was going to take them to a Giants game and,

2985
01:56:33.920 --> 01:56:35.860
we were gonna go and do all these fun things,

2986
01:56:35.860 --> 01:56:36.770
take them to the city.

2987
01:56:36.770 --> 01:56:37.603
They wanted to see a cable car and all,

2988
01:56:37.603 --> 01:56:40.550
they didn't believe that it gets pulled by a cable.

2989
01:56:40.550 --> 01:56:41.413
So I was gonna do all this stuff.

2990
01:56:41.413 --> 01:56:44.310
We spent every single day, all day at the boat,

2991
01:56:44.310 --> 01:56:47.540
getting it ready until literally the night before.

2992
01:56:47.540 --> 01:56:49.630
And so here we are just like you said,

2993
01:56:49.630 --> 01:56:52.110
we'll have broken all the rules,

2994
01:56:52.110 --> 01:56:57.110
but, in that time was spent with each other

2995
01:56:57.940 --> 01:56:59.727
and it was stressful, but it was together.

2996
01:56:59.727 --> 01:57:01.470
And there was a ton of trust

2997
01:57:01.470 --> 01:57:02.780
and community building happening.

2998
01:57:02.780 --> 01:57:07.780
And I remember like the night before my wife asking me

2999
01:57:08.520 --> 01:57:09.353
how I was feeling, cause you know

3000
01:57:09.353 --> 01:57:11.027
it was a very stressful week, two weeks leading up.

3001
01:57:11.027 --> 01:57:13.900
And I said, I can't wait until tomorrow morning.

3002
01:57:13.900 --> 01:57:15.210
All I have to do is push off.

