and they've made maybe even written some books on it,

3512
02:17:21.190 --> 02:17:23.390
but they have not been out there and tested that.

3513
02:17:23.390 --> 02:17:28.390
And when I, when I started to,

3514
02:17:28.560 --> 02:17:30.410
I've been doing this for over 15 years now,

3515
02:17:30.410 --> 02:17:33.480
I started teaching leadership

3516
02:17:33.480 --> 02:17:34.730
and how to build high-performance teams.

3517
02:17:34.730 --> 02:17:36.008
Yeah. I have my own process.

3518
02:17:36.008 --> 02:17:38.620
We have our steps and whatever you want to call it,

3519
02:17:38.620 --> 02:17:40.560
but it constantly changes.

3520
02:17:40.560 --> 02:17:42.067
So every time that we add another,

3521
02:17:42.067 --> 02:17:44.010
add another row or another adventure,

3522
02:17:44.010 --> 02:17:45.840
I'm constantly tweaking it because in the end,

3523
02:17:45.840 --> 02:17:47.140
it's just a living document.

3524
02:17:47.140 --> 02:17:49.240
There's no, there's no right way to build teams.

3525
02:17:49.240 --> 02:17:51.644
It's not the process, it's a process.

3526
02:17:51.644 --> 02:17:54.160
But the most important element is that we're,

3527
02:17:54.160 --> 02:17:55.460
I'm going out there.

3528
02:17:55.460 --> 02:17:57.630
I'm building teams for different adventures.

3529
02:17:57.630 --> 02:18:01.300
I'm using my process best I can, but I'm making mistakes.

3530
02:18:01.300 --> 02:18:03.910
I'm making concessions that I'm telling people,

3531
02:18:03.910 --> 02:18:05.720
you shouldn't make, I'm making mistakes out there,

3532
02:18:05.720 --> 02:18:07.130
constantly learning from them.

3533
02:18:07.130 --> 02:18:10.270
And I'm bringing all that experience to our clients.

3534
02:18:10.270 --> 02:18:12.240
And, they've got they come with me,

3535
02:18:12.240 --> 02:18:15.140
with their, what their business challenges

3536
02:18:15.140 --> 02:18:16.540
and I'm gonna help consult them.

3537
02:18:16.540 --> 02:18:20.530
But we also do our most popular programs

3538
02:18:20.530 --> 02:18:21.780
are our experiential programs

3539
02:18:21.780 --> 02:18:23.037
where we take people rowing on,

3540
02:18:23.037 --> 02:18:26.140
on a river or sailing the British Virgin islands.

3541
02:18:26.140 --> 02:18:29.090
And we say, all right we are gonna teach this

3542
02:18:29.090 --> 02:18:30.480
by essentially putting together a team

3543
02:18:30.480 --> 02:18:32.840
just like I had to do and putting you out in a,

3544
02:18:32.840 --> 02:18:36.280
in a chaotic controlled chaotic environment,

3545
02:18:36.280 --> 02:18:39.360
Debbie from marketing and like Joe from sales.

3546
02:18:39.360 --> 02:18:41.880
Yeah. But we, when we work with a lot of,

3547
02:18:41.880 --> 02:18:44.326
upper management and above it's it's,

3548
02:18:44.326 --> 02:18:46.245
but these are people that are either,

3549
02:18:46.245 --> 02:18:48.994
maybe at the lowest level of coming into for the first time

3550
02:18:48.994 --> 02:18:50.640
leadership responsibilities.

3551
02:18:50.640 --> 02:18:52.400
Maybe they've never really managed people

3552
02:18:52.400 --> 02:18:53.940
all the way up to people that are managing people

3553
02:18:53.940 --> 02:18:56.660
for a long time, but are doing it a certain way, their way.

3554
02:18:56.660 --> 02:18:59.880
And that maybe just needs access to different perspectives.

3555
02:18:59.880 --> 02:19:04.170
And all we're doing is creating an environment for those,

3556
02:19:04.170 --> 02:19:07.760
those ideas and those experiences to happen.

3557
02:19:07.760 --> 02:19:09.620
And we don't sit there and placate and say,

3558
02:19:09.620 --> 02:19:12.050
look how you rode well together and worked as a team,

3559
02:19:12.050 --> 02:19:13.670
we actually dig, were very academic.

3560
02:19:13.670 --> 02:19:16.490
So half that experiences are that time with them

3561
02:19:16.490 --> 02:19:18.080
is spent in that experience with the other half

3562
02:19:18.080 --> 02:19:22.410
is a very academic deconstruction of that experience.

3563
02:19:22.410 --> 02:19:25.130
Because that's essentially what I'm doing when I debrief

3564
02:19:25.130 --> 02:19:30.070
my own adventures is how can I, what mistakes did I make?

3565
02:19:30.070 --> 02:19:32.790
How can I learn from those mistakes and move forward?

3566
02:19:32.790 --> 02:19:35.220
And, that's essentially what we're doing.

3567
02:19:35.220 --> 02:19:36.090
We're constantly being students.

3568
02:19:36.090 --> 02:19:38.190
I mean, you're, you're saying I'm almost at my PhD,

3569
02:19:38.190 --> 02:19:40.210
which I appreciate, but I'm gonna try to go for another one.

3570
02:19:40.210 --> 02:19:41.910
Yeah. And okay.

3571
02:19:41.910 --> 02:19:43.960
Since you're going to go for another one,

3572
02:19:45.540 --> 02:19:50.350
how do you think about communicating principles

3573
02:19:50.350 --> 02:19:55.070
around things like discipline, consistency,

3574
02:19:55.070 --> 02:20:00.070
motivation, inspiration, living your life purposeful

3575
02:20:00.800 --> 02:20:03.350
and, seeking your passion in terms

3576
02:20:03.350 --> 02:20:05.160
that gets thrown a lot around a lot,

3577
02:20:05.160 --> 02:20:08.290
kind of in the space of what you do.

3578
02:20:08.290 --> 02:20:12.613
And I'm sure, in the kind of keynote circuit speaker world,

3579
02:20:12.613 --> 02:20:15.050
I think it's very confusing for a lot of people

3580
02:20:15.050 --> 02:20:17.610
because those words are used cavalierly.

3581
02:20:17.610 --> 02:20:21.060
So what do they mean to you and where do you think people

3582
02:20:21.060 --> 02:20:24.110
should focus their energies who are trying to kind of

3583
02:20:24.110 --> 02:20:26.240
unlock something greater within themselves,

3584
02:20:26.240 --> 02:20:28.180
but feel stymied or stuck,

3585
02:20:28.180 --> 02:20:32.643
or just unsure about how to bring expression to that?

3586
02:20:33.900 --> 02:20:36.740
I think, yeah and I do a fair number of keynote speeches,

3587
02:20:36.740 --> 02:20:38.870
and it's hard to connect with people on keynotes.

3588
02:20:38.870 --> 02:20:40.293
You're on, you're on a stage and you're,

3589
02:20:40.293 --> 02:20:42.390
you're either telling a story or you're,

3590
02:20:42.390 --> 02:20:44.650
you're giving some examples and some anecdotes,

3591
02:20:44.650 --> 02:20:47.980
but largely the best work that I have with people on those

3592
02:20:47.980 --> 02:20:50.170
is afterwards when you're having dinner with them

3593
02:20:50.170 --> 02:20:51.716
or you're talking off stage with them and then have our

3594
02:20:51.716 --> 02:20:54.130
programs where we get to spend a little bit more time

3595
02:20:54.130 --> 02:20:55.150
making some connections.

3596
02:20:55.150 --> 02:20:57.850
And I think the first thing is like,

3597
02:20:57.850 --> 02:20:59.190
is there a willingness to change?

3598
02:20:59.190 --> 02:21:01.453
And I think for a lot of people,

3599
02:21:02.940 --> 02:21:05.230
a willingness to be a better leader

3600
02:21:05.230 --> 02:21:08.460
or a better builder of teams comes from the idea

3601
02:21:08.460 --> 02:21:11.090
that you're okay with kind of exposing yourself

3602
02:21:11.090 --> 02:21:12.819
and opening up your books to everybody else.

3603
02:21:12.819 --> 02:21:16.040
Because I used to play with all these different definitions

3604
02:21:16.040 --> 02:21:18.410
of leadership, and I thought, well, I'm in leadership,

3605
02:21:18.410 --> 02:21:21.120
so I have to have this really complex definition.

3606
02:21:21.120 --> 02:21:22.730
And now it's just one word it's just trust.

3607
02:21:22.730 --> 02:21:23.710
That's my definition.

3608
02:21:23.710 --> 02:21:26.089 line:15%
So what's the definition of leadership? I say it's trust.

3609
02:21:26.089 --> 02:21:29.420 line:15%
In order to build trust with the people that you're leading

3610
02:21:29.420 --> 02:21:31.454 line:15%
and for them to build trust within themselves.

3611
02:21:31.454 --> 02:21:33.800
Like you have to open up your books and--

3612
02:21:33.800 --> 02:21:35.390
Honesty vulnerability.

3613
02:21:35.390 --> 02:21:36.400
Yeah, exactly.

3614
02:21:36.400 --> 02:21:40.480
And I think, I think one of the hardest things for us to do,

3615
02:21:40.480 --> 02:21:43.290
even though we talk about a lot and talk about a cliche is,

3616
02:21:43.290 --> 02:21:44.450
talking about our failures.

3617
02:21:44.450 --> 02:21:47.750
And so I spent a lot of time showing how much

3618
02:21:47.750 --> 02:21:48.970
we can learn from our failures.

3619
02:21:48.970 --> 02:21:50.610
And I think we just spent a lot of time doing that

3620
02:21:50.610 --> 02:21:52.330
just now on this talk.

3621
02:21:52.330 --> 02:21:55.890
And we, here's another cliche that drives me nuts

3622
02:21:55.890 --> 02:21:57.090
is that one where you hear,

3623
02:21:57.090 --> 02:21:59.180
it's not how many times you get knocked down

3624
02:21:59.180 --> 02:22:00.460
it's how many times you get back up.

3625
02:22:00.460 --> 02:22:04.935
And I find that to be a very unproductive cliche,

3626
02:22:04.935 --> 02:22:08.660
or at least incomplete at the very least.

3627
02:22:08.660 --> 02:22:11.294
And the reality is these days,

3628
02:22:11.294 --> 02:22:13.367
nobody gives a shit how many times you get back up?

3629
02:22:13.367 --> 02:22:16.123
Like it's just too much talent in this world.

3630
02:22:16.987 --> 02:22:19.790
There's so much competition. People are smart.

3631
02:22:19.790 --> 02:22:23.000
People are, people are getting smarter, faster

3632
02:22:23.000 --> 02:22:25.200
they want to learn more.

3633
02:22:25.200 --> 02:22:29.140
And I think we need to spend more time understanding

3634
02:22:29.140 --> 02:22:32.320
how we got knocked down, talking about being vulnerable,

3635
02:22:32.320 --> 02:22:36.100
being authentic with ourselves and with each other

3636
02:22:36.100 --> 02:22:40.230
so that when we do get back up, we're a tougher,

3637
02:22:40.230 --> 02:22:44.443
harder, stronger, smarter target to knock down again

3638
02:22:44.443 --> 02:22:47.658
because we need to be resilient.

3639
02:22:47.658 --> 02:22:49.930
This resiliency piece is so important,

3640
02:22:49.930 --> 02:22:52.200
but if all we're doing is proving to people

3641
02:22:52.200 --> 02:22:54.840
that we'll get back up I mean, we're really not getting,

3642
02:22:54.840 --> 02:22:56.770
we're not getting any anywhere with that.

3643
02:22:56.770 --> 02:23:00.850
So, if people are looking how to like really be,

3644
02:23:00.850 --> 02:23:02.940
be authentic leaders and how to kind of

3645
02:23:02.940 --> 02:23:04.220
take on some of these challenges,

3646
02:23:04.220 --> 02:23:06.329
I think it starts with this building the trust piece.

3647
02:23:06.329 --> 02:23:09.440
And I think you can spend a lot of time working on that.

3648
02:23:09.440 --> 02:23:10.940
So how would you begin that?

3649
02:23:13.080 --> 02:23:16.090
I think a lot of people, and the reason I asked that is

3650
02:23:16.090 --> 02:23:18.680
I think that I agree with you

3651
02:23:18.680 --> 02:23:20.440
completely on the vulnerability piece.

3652
02:23:20.440 --> 02:23:23.630
Like you cannot expect to engender trust

3653
02:23:23.630 --> 02:23:26.370
if you're not willing to open up your books

3654
02:23:26.370 --> 02:23:28.440
to use your phrasiology to be honest,

3655
02:23:28.440 --> 02:23:30.520
to admit where you went wrong.

3656
02:23:30.520 --> 02:23:33.080
I think a lot of people are intimidated to do that

3657
02:23:33.080 --> 02:23:34.500
or scared to do that.

3658
02:23:34.500 --> 02:23:38.460
It feels not safe, or it feels the kind of

3659
02:23:38.460 --> 02:23:41.010
conventional wisdom around that would be if I do,

3660
02:23:41.010 --> 02:23:43.310
if I do that, I'll appear weak

3661
02:23:43.310 --> 02:23:45.210
and that will be across purposes

3662
02:23:45.210 --> 02:23:48.110
with the trust that I'm trying to engender.

3663
02:23:48.110 --> 02:23:50.510
The truth is actually the opposite.

3664
02:23:50.510 --> 02:23:53.600
But for somebody who's never engaged in that process,

3665
02:23:53.600 --> 02:23:57.150
like, I guess you would have to say like start small,

3666
02:23:57.150 --> 02:24:02.010
but getting somebody off that ledge and into a mindset

3667
02:24:02.010 --> 02:24:04.070
where they're comfortable sharing on that level

3668
02:24:04.070 --> 02:24:06.480
with people who are underneath them

3669
02:24:06.480 --> 02:24:07.831
or their colleagues at work,

3670
02:24:07.831 --> 02:24:10.960
or even at home in their relationships,

3671
02:24:10.960 --> 02:24:15.350
I think is a tough row to hoe for many, many people.

3672
02:24:15.350 --> 02:24:17.060
And it keeps us stuck.

3673
02:24:17.060 --> 02:24:21.817
And it also imprisons us in our emotional states.

3674
02:24:21.817 --> 02:24:24.940
It paralyzes us from the growth

3675
02:24:24.940 --> 02:24:27.370
that we actually seek and aspire to.

3676
02:24:27.370 --> 02:24:28.460
Yeah.

3677
02:24:28.460 --> 02:24:30.540
Well, I think one of the things I've noticed about myself

3678
02:24:30.540 --> 02:24:33.359
is the more I've accomplished and the more of these,

3679
02:24:33.359 --> 02:24:36.330
of these rows, the less macho I become.

3680
02:24:36.330 --> 02:24:38.510
I feel that at first you feel the need,

3681
02:24:38.510 --> 02:24:40.560
like you're saying to kind of beat your chest

3682
02:24:40.560 --> 02:24:42.810
and to say, look at me, I'm on this pedestal.

3683
02:24:42.810 --> 02:24:45.100
And the reason why you're down there and I'm up here

3684
02:24:45.100 --> 02:24:47.600
is because I manage you, I lead you, I'm your boss.

3685
02:24:47.600 --> 02:24:50.020
And then it starts to become such a flat hierarchical

3686
02:24:50.020 --> 02:24:52.170
structure by the time you start to accomplish these things,

3687
02:24:52.170 --> 02:24:55.215
you all start to start to become on the same level.

3688
02:24:55.215 --> 02:24:57.760
And I think I've learned that a lot.

3689
02:24:57.760 --> 02:25:00.530
I felt so I felt so much the need,

3690
02:25:00.530 --> 02:25:02.770
like at Sonoma state to show that I am bigger

3691
02:25:02.770 --> 02:25:04.220
and stronger and you should be scared of me.

3692
02:25:04.220 --> 02:25:05.460
At one time, one of my teammates

3693
02:25:05.460 --> 02:25:08.040
says the only reason people row hard for you

3694
02:25:08.040 --> 02:25:08.873
is because they're scared of you

3695
02:25:08.873 --> 02:25:10.230
not because they respect you.

3696
02:25:10.230 --> 02:25:11.830
And that really hit me hard.

3697
02:25:11.830 --> 02:25:13.320
And so I've been thinking about that--

3698
02:25:13.320 --> 02:25:14.490
Which works in the short term,

3699
02:25:14.490 --> 02:25:15.390
but not in the long term.

3700
02:25:15.390 --> 02:25:18.060
Exactly. Yeah. It's the stick vs the carrot thing.

3701
02:25:18.060 --> 02:25:20.630
So if people are looking for a way to start, as you said,

3702
02:25:20.630 --> 02:25:24.210
a jumping off point, I start with storytelling.

3703
02:25:24.210 --> 02:25:28.110
Start figuring out what kind of stories you can be telling.

3704
02:25:28.110 --> 02:25:30.960
Forget about showing your failings for a second,

3705
02:25:30.960 --> 02:25:33.320
or, deciding to say, oh, this I'm gonna tell them

3706
02:25:33.320 --> 02:25:34.267
this is where I failed.

3707
02:25:34.267 --> 02:25:36.610
This is gonna be a big thing. Forget about that.

3708
02:25:36.610 --> 02:25:38.992
Just start, start storytelling, start a narrative.

3709
02:25:38.992 --> 02:25:42.600
Every time I'm telling a story, giving a keynote speech,

3710
02:25:42.600 --> 02:25:44.980
even if it's just with friends at a dinner,

3711
02:25:44.980 --> 02:25:47.391
or if it's on a stage in front of thousands of people,

3712
02:25:47.391 --> 02:25:49.170
I make sure that every sentence

3713
02:25:49.170 --> 02:25:51.510
I'm saying is for the benefit of the person listening.

3714
02:25:51.510 --> 02:25:52.830
Otherwise you just bloviating

3715
02:25:52.830 --> 02:25:54.330
and talking about something about yourself,

3716
02:25:54.330 --> 02:25:57.450
but if you can have other people see themselves

3717
02:25:57.450 --> 02:25:58.590
in the stories that you tell

3718
02:25:58.590 --> 02:26:01.480
you're on the road to starting those conversations

3719
02:26:01.480 --> 02:26:03.960
that are going to talk about the shortcomings.

3720
02:26:03.960 --> 02:26:05.340
And that's a way to weight in shallow,

3721
02:26:05.340 --> 02:26:06.870
because you're not, I'm not saying you to say,

3722
02:26:06.870 --> 02:26:08.090
this is where I made a mistake.

3723
02:26:08.090 --> 02:26:09.150
You're just telling a story.

3724
02:26:09.150 --> 02:26:11.630
And that encourages people to be like,

3725
02:26:11.630 --> 02:26:12.883
I think this one time,

3726
02:26:12.883 --> 02:26:15.610
you've given a fair enough fair amount of keynotes as well,

3727
02:26:15.610 --> 02:26:18.540
but you talk to people off stage and they want to come

3728
02:26:18.540 --> 02:26:21.180
and shake your hand and share stuff with you.

3729
02:26:21.180 --> 02:26:24.058
And I've never really identified or connected with

3730
02:26:24.058 --> 02:26:26.470
the macho guys that come up to me and say, oh,

3731
02:26:26.470 --> 02:26:28.240
I'm just like you, I hate losing.

3732
02:26:28.240 --> 02:26:29.360
I never, I never lose.

3733
02:26:29.360 --> 02:26:31.330
Which first of all, I lose all the time

3734
02:26:31.330 --> 02:26:33.140
so I don't identify with that.

3735
02:26:33.140 --> 02:26:35.239
But I just feel like they missed the whole point.

3736
02:26:35.239 --> 02:26:37.279
But one that happened a few years ago

3737
02:26:37.279 --> 02:26:38.490
that always stuck with me.

3738
02:26:38.490 --> 02:26:40.740
And I'll never remember what her name was

3739
02:26:40.740 --> 02:26:42.610
and I can picture her though.

3740
02:26:42.610 --> 02:26:46.200
And she wanted to share a story of her and her twin sister.

3741
02:26:46.200 --> 02:26:49.800
They were, they had stopped talking for years and years.

3742
02:26:49.800 --> 02:26:51.680
And moved away from each other.

3743
02:26:51.680 --> 02:26:54.900
And they finally got reconnected and put their past away.

3744
02:26:54.900 --> 02:26:56.260
And they decided they're gonna have,

3745
02:26:56.260 --> 02:26:58.450
do a century bike ride together.

